CinefexSearchData.register("text/36",{"issue":36,"date":"November 1988","pages":[["1","Cover","number 36 $5.50 Canada - $7.50 cine ex"],["2","Closing pages",""],["3","Contents","cinefex CINEFEX 36 - November 1988. Published quarterly at P.O. Box 20027, Riverside, California 92516. Subscriptions (U.S.): four issues - $17; eight issues - $28; twelve issues - $40. Return postage must accompany all unsolicited manuscripts, photos or other materials. Printed by Valley Printers, Riverside, California; color separations by ViviColour, City of Industry, California. Contents copyright © 1988 by Don Shay. All rights reserved. Reproduction in whole or in part without written permission is prohibited. ...the journal of cinematic illusions editor & publisher Don Shay associate editor Jody Duncan Shannon contributing editors Janine Pourroy Adam Eisenberg editorial assistant Ann Dredla A Planetful of Aliens To fulfill a need for hundreds of extraterrestrial characters - ranging from principal players to incidental extras - the producers of Alien Nation turned to Stan Winston Studios. There - under the direction of Alec Gillis, Shane Mahan, John Rosengrant and Tom Woodruff, with onset collaboration by Zoltan Elek - was mounted the largest makeup effects show in twenty years. Article by Ron Magid. Exaggerated Reality Even with near-total access to a brand new high-rise office building, the makers of Die Hard needed something more to bring their action thriller to the screen. To simulate a bomb blast in an elevator shaft and a giant rooftop explosion and helicopter crash, producer Joel Silver enlisted the services of visual effects producer Richard Edlund and his Boss Film Corporation. Article by Adam Eisenberg. Double Vision On Dead Ringers - the latest chiller by horror impresario David Cronenberg - optical effects supervisor Lee Wilson worked with Balsmeyer and Everett and Film Effects of Toronto to create a new generation of split-screen opticals that enabled actor Jeremy Irons to play scenes with himself as twin brothers without the customary restriction of stationary splits or even locked-off cameras. Article by Don Shay. The Right Blob for the Right Job For his updated remake of The Blob, director Chuck Russell engaged visual effects production supervisor Michael Fink to oversee an outpouring of cinematic illusions that included gooey creature effects by Lyle Conway and Stuart Ziff, special makeup creations by Tony Gardner and opticals and miniature photography by Hoyt Yeatman and Dream Quest Images. Article by Robert G. Pielke. FRONT COVER - Beverly Mantle lies lifeless at the conclusion of Dead Ringers. Alien renegade William Harcourt makes a last stand in Alien Nation - BACK COVER. 4 18 32 50"],["4","Alien Nation","A PLANETFUL OF ALIENS"],["5","Alien Nation","Science fiction films rarely deal with entire alien populations since creating hundreds or thousands of extraterrestrials is expensive, time-consuming and, even today, extremely difficult to do convincingly. Consequently the genre tends to specialize in token representation, with one or two nonhumans on board the Enterprise standing in as a form of economic shorthand for an entire race of alien beings. For this reason, the standard by which all other epic makeup films are measured remains the first - Twentieth Century-Fox's 1968 excursion to the Planet of the Apes. Exactly twenty years later, Fox has picked up its own gauntlet by presenting Alien Nation, a thinly veiled allegory about racism in which humankind finds itself playing host to thousands of refugees from another planet who have been forced to immigrate to ours. A peaceful, if uneasy, coexistence prevails, though the alien 'newcomers' are quickly relegated to second class status. Like other minorities, the aliens live in their own barrios, frequent their own bars and nightclubs and develop their own underground. And like other minorities in social environments both new and hostile, some engage willingly in crime while others turn to law enforcement. Triggering the plot of Alien Nation is a ghetto murder with far-reaching implications. Investigating are two Los Angeles cops - one a racist human played by James Caan and the other an alien officer portrayed by Mandy Patinkin. To solve the crime - and uncover the monstrous conspiracy behind it - the two must overcome innate mistrust and prejudice and learn to work together no matter how alien their appearances and behavior may seem to the other. As scripted by RockneS. O'Bannon, Alien Nation - originally titled Future Tense - is a science fictional retelling of In the Heat of the Night. In fact, the working title under which it was shot - Outer Heat - was a tongue-in-cheek amalgam of In the Heat of the Night and Outer Limits. Though predominantly an action picture, it is also a socially conscious throwback to such landmark genre films as Silent Runningand Planet of the Apes. Coproducing the film was Gale Anne Hurd whose previous ventures into science fiction include such hugely successful projects as The Terminator and Aliens. \"The screenplay was originally submitted to me on spec - one of twenty or twentyfive that we receive every week. The agency representing it outlined the general idea and asked me to read it very quickly since it was also being sent to other producers at various studios. Ellen Collett, my director of development, and I read it that evening and both of us had the same response - it was a real page-turner and neither of us was able to put it down. ParAlien Nation photographs copyright © 1988 by Twentieth Century Fox Film Corporation. All rights reserved. Production unit still photography by Jane O'Neal. Additional photography courtesy of Stan Winston Studios. Special thanks to Arlene Marechal, Bette Einbinder and Dale Berliner. ticularly what piqued my interest was the whole approach to the immigrant experience and the extrapolation of that as an allegory in a science fiction setting.\" Working with coproducer Richard Kobritz - who was already attached to the project - Hurd secured funding through Fox and began casting about for an experienced makeup team that could reasonably be expected to bring an alien nation into being. \"We knew from the start that it was going to be an enormous undertaking from a makeup standpoint. The primary problem was how to sell an alien race that was humanoid without making it look like people in rubber suits - and how to make it affordable. We knew there was going to be a lot of makeup application time and removal time - that was a given. But this is an action picture and it was picturnat was pron important for us that we not end up with makeup that was prone to smearing and tearing and degraded so quickly after its application that we would spend more time on the makeup than we would on the shooting.\" Although several makeup effects companies were considered, the assignment fell - perhaps inevitably - to the Stan Winston Studios where a solid working relationship had already been forged on both The Terminator and Aliens. Winston himself, however, would not be directly involved. Instead, the design of the aliens and the logistics of running the show would be left to the care of his four top artists - Alec Gillis, Shane Mahan, John Rosengrant and Tom Woodruff - an approach Winston had employed with success on Pumpkinhead, his debut film as a director. The arrangement constitutes an unusual set of golden handcuffs -affording Winston the opportunity to branch out and diversify his talents while insuring his effects team continued employment coupled with greater responsibility and increased recognition for individual effort. Without hesitation, the Winston crew members welcomed Alien Nation as an opportunity to tackle a show involving strictly foam latex appliance makeups. After years of dealing with mechanical monsters - from the Invaders from Mars to the Predator - the boys in the lab craved nostalgia. They wanted to make their own Planet of the Apes. In the process, they got far more than they bargained for - and also far less. Alien Nation turned out to be the biggest makeup show in twenty years, and the whole thing had to be designed, sealed and delivered within six months - roughly half the preproduction time allotted to its simian predecessor. \"The four of us - Tom Woodruff, John Rosengrant, Shane Mahan and I - were the alien effects coordinators,\" Alec Gillis related. \"We were involved in everything from designing and sculpting the aliens to supervising a crew of about thirty people. We sculpted the main characters, supervised the molding and production of their appliances and oversaw the sculpting of the secondary and background characters.\" After several meetings with Gale Anne Hurd, director Graham Baker and Stan Winston, the alien coordinators began making dozens of preliminary article by design sketches. \"At first we went off and made our own Ron Magid sketches,\" Tom Woodruff recalled. \"Then we got together and"],["6","Alien Nation","mismatched antagonists as partners has seldom been taken to the extremes of Alien Nation - a science detective Matthew Sykes (James Caan) is partnered with extraterrestrial officer Patinkin) in a case involving murder and drugs in nearfuture Los Angeles. The year is 1991, and earth is playing host to some 300,000 alien 'newcomers' - humanoid engineered for slave labor - who have been shipwrecked in the Mojave Desert and gradually assimilated into the fabric of society. / In the county morgue, Francisco assistant discover sinister signs of a virulent narcotic unidentified alien gunman. corpse - detailed with alien facial appliances glued onto a lifecast of the stuntman sculpted by Mike Tric and Greg Figiel. More than thirty artists and technicians were engaged at Stan Winston prosthetic makeups and alien body parts required The tried-and-true cop film practice of pairing fiction thriller in which human supremacist Sam Francisco (Mandy beings genetically and a newcomer lab on the body of an The latex and polyfoam playing the role - was Studios to produce the enormous volume of for the film. 6 - CINEFEX 36 compared drawings and did tracings of certain parts in order to put together a composite character. Most of the designing on paper fell to Alec Gillis, John Rosengrant and me, while Shane Mahan worked out some problems in clay.\" \"We went through a lot of design phases,\" Gillis added. \"I think the earlier designs tended to be more outrageous, but it was decided that the look shouldn't be too 'over the top.' The script described the aliens as having leathery skin leaning toward the reptilian - and none of them were under six feet tall, so at first they were all hulking creatures. It's hard to say how many designs we did - maybe six different designs on paper and another five when we started sculpting, narrowing it along the way, taking some of the lumps and bumps off until it was smooth and refined. We always wanted to create something that looked as if it might be another variation on the human species with a bit of extra texture. We wanted to make the features a little cruder, and we tried various things such as moving the nostrils and other features to slightly different places.\" Resisting attempts to accentuate the alien elements, Baker and his producers held firm in their insistence that the 'newcomers' be made to look as human as possible. For the makeup crew, artistic interpretation was not the only issue involved. \"If for no other reason,\" John Rosengrant admitted, \"we felt that the alien approach would be easier because the appliances would be thicker and therefore simpler to make and apply.\" More concerned with concept than convenience, the director and producers continued deemphasizing the alien aspects and insisting upon greater subtlety. \"We wanted the aliens to be more like a different ethnic race than like lizard people,\" Hurd explained. \"That was a very conscious decision. We didn't want our audiences thinking, 'Gee, look how different these aliens are.' Rather, after about five minutes we wanted the audience to accept them as different from us, but not so different that no one is buying the storyline. We wanted the aliens to be characters - not creatures.\" Based on approved design sketches, Shane Mahan and Shannon Shea commenced sculpting a series of masks - three male and three female - to be worn by background characters in the numerous crowd scenes. Even here there were some differences in interpretation. \"Our clay models started out a bit more frightening looking than they ended up,\" said Mahan, \"and they had a lot more texture. But the producers and director thought they were a bit too ugly. We went through a phase where the males were going to have spines on top of their heads - kind of like a rooster's comb - and when they got angry, these spines would raise up. That would've required a mechanical headpiece, and Graham Baker thought it was a little too extreme so we went with smooth heads instead. Even without the rooster combs, the background characters turned out to be more alien than the featured principals. \"The background designs look most like our original characters,\" Woodruff explained. \"They had heavier brows and a heavier skin texture that looked more leathery, and there were more pronounced lumps on the backs of their heads. After we went into production on these masks, Graham Baker decided that he wanted the aliens to look even more subtle, so we streamlined the design once we started doing the principal characters. At that point we had already made about a hundred background masks, but we decided to go ahead and use them since they were going to be far away in most of the shots and would not cause a problem.\" \"Ultimately, the aliens had very smooth skin and almost featureless faces,\" said Mahan. \"Their eyebrows were flattened, their noses were broadened, they had no ears and their heads were larger than a human's. The director didn't want them to look menacing since they're basically benevolent creatures. He wanted the makeups to be subdued in order to allow the character of the actor to read through the rubber pieces.\" Baker's insistence upon greater and greater subtlety was rooted in his fear that audiences would be alienated by the characters instead of identifying with them. \"He wanted people to immediately take a liking to the aliens,\" added Rosengrant. \"Since they're on screen for most of the movie and most of them are sympathetic, I don't know that I completely agree with his desire to make them as human as possible - but I have to admit they worked. When I went out to the set and saw the aliens walking around, I got a really neat feeling because they actually seemed like a real race of people.\" As the alien effects coordinators struggled to finalize concepts and commence full-scale production, attendant efforts were underway to enlist a top-notch makeup artist to supervise the application of the alien prosthetics on set. At the top of Gale Anne Hurd's list was Zoltan Elek - a veteran makeup artist whose work with Michael Westmore on Mask had earned an Oscar in 1985. \"Obviously the only way a makeup design will work on set is if the person who is applying it every day and touching it up can carry out the intent of what has been planned and developed and tested - perhaps even prior to his or her involvement. Zoltan came recommended by Stan Winston - they had worked together on numerous occasions. In fact, we had tried to get him for The Terminator, but he was doing Mask at the time. When we started this project, Zoltan was working on Max Headroom, but he was able to come in and consult with us while we were designing the makeups and discuss with us application problems that we might encounter.\" Though he enjoyed a longstanding working relationship with Winston - most recently on Monster Squad - Elek balked at what he perceived to be another monster movie. At this point in his career, what he wanted to do were character makeups - which Hurd insisted was exactly what she and Graham Baker had in mind for Alien Nation. Not easily swayed, Elek had to see for himself. 'The first time I went to Stan's lab to look at the alien designs I was not too hot on them,\" Elek admitted. \"Monster Squad was fun, but I didn't want to do another film like that right away and this show looked to me like another monster movie. When I went in to see Gale, I told her, 'I really don't know if I want to do this show unless the aliens are made"],["7","Alien Nation","to resemble humans more closely - which means much more delicate work for me than making monsters.' Gale insisted that the designs were being changed and I told her, In that case, I'm available.' Ultimately she and the director kept changing things until they almost pushed the aliens too far in the opposite direction - they're almost too humanl Their facial structure is completely different, but they still remind us so much of ourselves that we can believe them - which is how I saw them when I first read the script.\" Ironically, the very changes Elek insisted upon were to cause him considerable aggravation during the shoot, creating one of the most difficult challenges of his career. Once a generic alien concept was agreed upon and finalized, each of the alien effects coordinators selected a specific area to supervise from beginning to end. Shane Mahan opted to handle the Sam Francisco makeup for lead actor Mandy Patinkin. John Rosengrant took the supporting character of Cassandra - a female alien and exotic dancer played by Leslie Bevis - and also supervised the small alien son of Francisco. Tom Woodruff selected the character of William Harcourt - an evil alien drug trafficker fronting as a respectable businessman - played by Terence Stamp. Late in the film, Harcourt overdoses on the mind- and body-altering drug he is attempting to manufacture and peddle to his own people - necessitating a transformation suit which became Alec Gillis' primary responsiblity. Though certain areas overlapped and the full assignment extended far beyond the principal characters, the supervisory compartmentalization eased the workload considerably. Sculpting for the principal makeups was technically straightforward. A full head lifecast was taken of each actor, over which the alien appliances were fashioned in clay. Virtually all of the makeups were conceived originally in seven pieces - one each for the neck, chin and forehead, one for each cheek, a combined nose and upper lip piece and a headpiece encasing the ears and the back of the head. The challenge for the artists was to accurately capture the personality of each actor in his or her own makeup. \"That was one of the most important things for us,\" Mahan insisted. \"We wanted the audience to be able to recognize the actors immediately. They were all so good, it would have been a shame to cover them up completely.\" With appliances covering nearly every major facial feature, the task was not as simple as it might seem. \"We were very fortunate,\" remarked Gillis, \"in that a lot of the actors had great faces to begin with. The actor who played Trent Porter, for example - Brian Thompson - was a really rugged guy, so he came off looking like a really rough character. On the other hand, Kevyn Major Howard - who played Harcourt's murderous henchman - had very delicate, boyish kind of features, so we used that quality to create a creepy juxtaposition.\" Even with the alien physiognomy firmly established, other changes continued to plague the makeup unit. When Zoltan Elek came in to do his first makeup test, he argued insistently that the alien coloration should be much closer to human tones CINEFEX 36 • 7"],["8","Alien Nation","than the yellowish pigment already established. \"Stan didn't agree with me fully in the beginning,\" Elek recalled. \"He felt the aliens should be a totally different color than the humans, with a lot of yellows, blues and grays. To me, that scheme was too monstrous - in fact, we ended up using it on the Harcourt monster at the climax. But when I did the first makeup test - and this is one of the reasons I love Stan - I had a long talk with him and convinced him to give my ideas a chance. He said, 'Okay, you're doing the test, so this whole coloring thing is in your hands.' I did my first makeup test on Brian Thompson, who plays the son of an old alien killed in a liquor store shooting. My first color scheme came out too warm - he ended up looking like a redneck from Texas - but the producer, director and cameraman liked the general concept, even though they wanted it toned down. Luckily this meant I was on the right track from the first test. Unfortunately, this blew off Stan who already had some ninety background masks finished and painted. The masks were of a yellowish color, while the tone we went with looked more orange to the naked eye and photographed exactly like human skin.\" As any makeup artist will attest, the most difficult prosthetic makeup is one worn by a beautiful woman who must still look beautiful. In the history of special makeup effects, only a handful of these makeups have ever been successfully handled - the most recent examples being the fine work by Tom and Bari Burman on Nastassia Kinski in Cat People and Anjelica Huston in Captain Eo. Now John Rosengrant can add his transformation of actress Leslie Bevis into the alien stripper Cassandra to this select number of well-realized female characters. \"It was hard work,\" Rosengrant affirmed. \"Going into it, creating the female aliens seemed like it was going to be much easier than it was. To duplicate the softness of a woman's face and skin texture - first in clay and then in foam latex - became a very tough job. You would think just duplicating a female's face would be simple, but it was one of the most demanding sculptures I have ever had to do. Making a beautiful woman beautiful gave me a helluva timel\" Rosengrant made his first clay sketch over the most unlikely of lifecasts. \"I took an old cast Stan had lying around of Mary Tyler Moore and tried to give her face an alien look without making it ugly. I gave her a different, more pronounced bone structure and also flared and swept back her nostrils. Graham Baker took a look at my clay sketch and felt it was the right direction to go in, so I just happened to fall into doing the female alien.\" Once Rosengrant was able to sculpt on Leslie Bevis' own lifecast, Baker requested that the female alien be made even more subtle. \"I must have resculpted her three times just to get the right smoothness and a nice fleshy look. I kept bringing the pieces closer to a human face, making changes in the nose, the cheekbones, everywhere, to make it very subtle. Once it was cast, we did a makeup test which just was not satisfactory, so I went back and made some more changes. Fortunately, even though we were working on a very tight sched8 * CINEFEX 36 HEADS"],["9","Alien Nation","ule, I had a few weeks left before my character started working, so I was able to do it the way I like to see things done - I made a prototype, ironed out the problems and then remade it for real. When we did the second makeup test, everything was fine and dandyl\" Another makeup test for a sequence in which the Cassandra character does an exotic striptease did not go so well. \"We constructed an entirely new body for her out of appliances,\" Gillis said. \"We made a set of breast pieces to enlarge her bust, a stomach piece with a navel that went down just below her belt line, new shoulder blades, a more protruding spine and a small tail. It was made entirely of foam latex, and the test makeup - applied to an actress other than Leslie Bevis - really looked great. At first Graham was really pleased with it, but after mulling it over he decided the safest thing to do was not to use it. He felt the makeup was becoming too grotesque and would take away from Cassandra's beauty. He was very wary about turning the audience off from these characters, so the suit fell by the wayside. They ended up just putting body makeup on her - and since she has a nice body, it worked fine.\" Based on his experience in creating the Cassandra facial makeup, Rosengrant supervised the sculpting - by David Anderson and John Blake - of two other minor female characters. In a similar vein, he oversaw the production of the alien makeup for child actor Brian Lando - playing Francisco's son - another extremely difficult assignment. \"Once again we were faced with the problems I had doing the female makeup - children are very soft and smooth and textureless. Fortunately we were able to start with a lifecast of the actor which gave the proper proportions - the shape of the skull, the small nose and puffy little cheeks. It was a matter of taking distinguishing alien features and scaling them down to fit a child's face.\" After the principal makeups were sculpted, each had to be molded - and molded and molded. Mandy Patinkin, for example, had to have enough prosthetics for three months of shooting - some ninety sets of appliances \"We were fighting a time crunch,\" Mahan related. \"In order to make all of the pieces required, I had to duplicate every single positive and negative mold part for each appliance many times. There was no way we could have made all the pieces we needed with just one set. I can't even begin to give an accurate count of how many molds there were, but we must have ended up with at least seventy individual molds - just for Mandy. His makeup was the most extreme case - for that one I had to make six or seven molds for each piece - though Terence Stamp's molds also had to be duplicated a few times, as did many of the other characters'.\" Remaking prosthetic molds is an art in itself, requiring great precision to insure that the duplicates are as accurate to the sculpture as the originals. In order to insure against the generational loss of size and detail that would occur if appliances from the original molds were cast, Mahan injected liquid Roma clay into a silicone mold of the original sculpture. He then finessed this clay press, carefully blending it down onto Patinkin's lifecast to insure that the thickness of the duplicate pieces exactly the same on the original and the duplicate pieces, Mandy's face might appear to swell and shrink from shot to shot. Once the clay press was touched up, I recapped each of the pieces with a stone mold. Making each piece six times took quite a long while to do.\" Heavily taxed to supply even one set of appliances per day for each principal actor, the Winston crew found their workload multiplied as production began and the on-set application crew - headed by Zoltan Elek - began demanding backup copies for each day's shooting, \"Because actors need breaks and because of problems with timing,\" Mahan explained, \"the on-set guys requested double makeups - which meant we had to send out two sets at a time instead of onel That was difficult, but we did it somehow. There was no time to do anything extra, however. Whenever there were little glitches in the pieces - like air bubbles, for example, where we would normally not use those pieces - we would have to send them to the set anyway. it was either that or nothing.\" \"One of the challenges of this job,\" Gillis added, \"was the limited amount of time for research and development. Under normal conditions, any one of these characters would have taken several months to do. On this project, we had to break our schedule down and allow just two-and-a-half weeks to get each character into full production - including duplicating the molds! There was no time to go back and deal with problems. We really had to crank them out. The worst part was that everything we did had to go before the camera as it was. It was really a rough show from that point of view.\" Hard pressed as always, it soon became apparent to the effects coordinators that there would not be time to create individual character makeups for day players. Taking their cue from a system Kenny Myers developed for Return of the Living Dead II, the alien unit decided to use mix-and-match makeups on bit actors. \"We would grab a head from this one and a chin from that one and so on until we'd put something together for each character,\" Mahan recalled. \"The system had its obvious problems, but it worked okay - especially when we had our big street scenes where there were supposed to be hundreds of aliens. We wanted to have a nice gradation between our principal characters and our background characters.\" The greatest single technical failing of Planet of the Apes is the lack of gradation between the wonderful principal makeups and the mediocre background masks - a problem the Alien Nation crew sought to avoid. \"We had what we referred to as 'A-B-C-D' makeups,\" Gillis explained. \"The 'A' makeups were those custom designed for the principal actors, while the 'B' makeups were mix-and-match where we would recombine pieces from other actors to create a new character. The 'C' and 'D' makeups were just background masks. Our 'D' masks - the ones furthest from camera - were overhead slip latex masks incapable of changing expression. The 'C' masks, however, were Since Stan Winston was unable to involve himself personally in the project, supervision of the massive makeup effort fell to his four principal artists - Alec Gillis, Shane Mahan, John Rosengrant and Tom Woodruff. Tasked with creating literally hundreds of aliens - and with only six months in which to prepare - the effects team devised four distinct makeup types ranging from full prosthetic appliances for featured players to simple latex masks for background extras. Shane Mahan examines his nearly finished sculpture of Sam Francisco fashioned in clay over a lifecast of Mandy Patinkin. / At work on the Trent Porter character - whose father is slain in the opening minimarket holdup - Alec Gillis scribes lines into an alienized rendering of actor Brian Thompson to delineate where the multipiece appliance sections will be joined. CINEFEX 36 • 9"],["10","Alien Nation","During preproduction, the makeup coordinators were director Graham Baker and the producers to refine and revise their alien concepts Originally the aliens were to already been finished prior to the design change. Matt Rose airbrushes one of the ruthless killer in the employ pieces painstakingly applied and blended on set by Zoltan seasoned makeup artists. / Kipling prepares to execute the hapless proprietor of a rough section of the alien continually urged by - making them subtly more human and less extraterrestrial with each successive alteration. be somewhat more serpentine in form and coloration - a look that would prevail to some degree since more than a hundred of the slip-on background masks had completed masks. / Actor Kevyn Major Howard undergoes the tedious process required to transfigure him into a of alien drug czar William Harcourt. Most of the characters requiring prosthetic makeups were covered with four basic Elek and a contingent of mini-market located in a barrio in Los Angeles. 10 * CINEFEX 36 made of foam latex and could be glued down to get some expression from the characters in the middleground.\" Soon appliances and masks were being manufactured on a production line basis to insure that the vast demand for pieces would be met. \"That was the biggest chore,\" Mahan affirmed, \"trying to keep up with the demand. We had a mass production system going on where one person would be assigned to do all the heads, for example. We would pull the pieces out of the oven, fix them up, paint them and have them ready for pickup that afternoon to be taken to the set. The pieces were barely cold when Zoltan and his crew applied them.\" In this type of high-speed mass production, the headpieces promised to be the Achilles heel that might bring the whole manufacturing process to a standstill. Making those pieces took about as long as any other, but painting them was another matter. Mike Spatola headed up the painting crew charged with maintaining consistency on the many hundreds of prosthetic pieces churned out for the show. Although the aliens were hairless by design, the coordinators decided that they should have spotted skin where hair would be on a human. Thus each alien headpiece had to have a custom-painted spotting pattern that matched with precision to every other headpiece made for that particular character. The situation looked grim until someone came up with a revolutionary idea. \"We made vacuformed shells off a plaster copy of each head appliance,\" Gillis revealed, \"then cut out holes in the plastic pieces where the spots would be. It really saved us a helluva lot of time, especially since we could not reuse the headpieces as we had hoped. There was no way to remove them in one piece, so we had to produce a new headpiece for each day's shooting. These templates really saved the day, and I think there's a lot of potential uses for them in During one of his first tests, Elek added another unexpected element. \"Even though the aliens were supposed to be bald with only those patterns on their heads, I felt that if we did not add some other texture they would look too rubbery. Stan was one hundred percent against the idea until I tried using chopped hair which produced just a very slight stubble. In fact, when the characters were photographed from the front or the side, you could not actually see the hair - you could only sense a kind of texture on their heads. If the aliens were backlit you could see it, but even then all you could detect was a little fuzz.\" Sadly, some aspects of the alien makeups had to be discarded because they were too uncomfortable for the actors or too timeconsuming for the lab crew to make. Contact lenses for the aliens were immediately ruled out because of the large numbers of actors involved, all of whom would have to be specially fitted. Expense aside, it was concluded that the actors would have enough trouble wearing the elaborate makeups without the added burden of lenses. Also quick to fall by the wayside were special hand appliances designed to be muscular in appearance and cover each actor's hands and forearms. Though a production line was begun to start manufacturing the pieces,"],["11","Alien Nation","CINEFEX 36 • 11"],["12","Alien Nation","12 - CINEFEX 36"],["13","Alien Nation","ultimately these too were discarded to minimize performer discomfort. Instead of appliances, each actor's hands were covered with makeup and detailed on the backs with a few spots.\" As the work progressed, Zoltan Elek began to realize with growing anxiety that in asking for subtler makeups, he had unintentionally created an almost impossible task for himself. \"Ninety percent of each makeup was totally free of wrinkles or large features - they were very smooth. With appliance makeups that smooth, it meant that I could not afford to make any mistakes along the blending edge as I applied it because any error would break up the smoothness of the surface and betray the fact that it was a makeup. Also, the way the makeups were broken down was very complex and each piece went on in an unusual way. During the tests we very carefully developed a system of applying each piece. It took a long time and a lot of sweat to figure this system out.\" Due to the special difficulties the alien appliances presented, each makeup artist Elek hired had to be trained to apply the complex prosthetics. \"This was definitely not a regular appliance show. Each time I brought in extra help I had to have them come in a day early to watch how the makeups were done and to explain everything so the next day they would know what to do. Because of the complexity of the makeup, it was impossible for even the best makeup artist in the world to come in cold and do it.\" At first reading, Elek failed to realize just how many aliens would be called for by the script, \"Once the makeups were finalized during the tests, I realized I had no idea how we were going to handle all the people we had to make up - especially since we had to train everyone to do this. When we were shooting both first and second unit, I had to have seventeen makeup artists - including two hairdressers and a body makeup person - in order to handle the workload.\" The intensely demanding nature of the alien makeups created one unforeseen and exciting benefit for the entire production. \"Every single makeup artist who came to help us wanted to come back, because where else could they get this much experience? John Elliott, Michael Mills and Monty Westmore were my principal assistants, but everyone on my crew wanted to have a chance to try making up each character because they all turned out to have their own unique set of problems. Makeup artists love that. It's the best way to learn - when you have to try different things and use all your skill to figure out how to solve a problem immediately. Many times we did not even have time to test the makeups - we just had to do them - but that was the fun of it.\" When the effects coordinators discovered how complicated their makeups were to apply, they tried to find ways to simplify them by condensing the number of pieces. \"Since the overlapping pieces did not move as we had hoped,\" Alec Gillis noted, \"we ended up combining several pieces to make one big appliance that would cover half of an actor's face. Thus we were able to condense many of our original seven-piece makeups into four pieces - two large face pieces in conjunction with the headpiece and the neck piece. Of course we had to remold the makeups to do it. Just when we realized we could get away with doing the makeups this way, Shane finished his work on Mandy Patinkin's prosthetics. Things got so fast and furious that we never had a chance to redesign that makeup, so Mandy always had to wear the more difficult seven-piece makeup.\" As unfortunate as this was for Patinkin, it was many times worse for Elek who had the dubious honor of applying those complex pieces day after day. Three-and-a-half to four-and-ahalf hours per application were required to complete the process, which Elek proclaimed to be the toughest makeup job of his career. \"I first applied Mandy's baldcap, which covered his hair and provided an anchor to secure the headpiece. Next I applied a full neck piece which extended from his shoulders up to his chin. Once that was in place, I attached the headpiece which covered the back of his head and ears, ending a little higher than his hairline in front. Since his ears were glued in practically airtight, sometimes I would have him test the makeup by shutting his lips, pinching his nostrils and blowing air out his ears If he felt a leak anywhere, I knew the headpiece was not secure. The chin piece was next. That ended just below his lip line - which is a hard blend to accomplish. The toughest piece I've ever worked with was Mandy's nose piece. It was so thin and the rubber was so soft that if I put the adhesive on and slipped by a hair as I was pressing the piece down, I would push a wrinkle into it that I would never be able to get out - I'd have to throw the whole thing away. It was so tough that even though I applied it more than sixty times, I always said, 'Oh God, please be with me now'- and I really meant it. Finally I applied the \"Blending these seven pieces together seamlessly was the hardest part of the whole job, because the key to these makeups was their smoothness. Once they were blended perfectly, I could not afford a single mistake in the coloring because it would read right away - like a blemish on a clean sheet of paper. The toughest blend of all was between the cheek piece and Mandy's eyes. The skin around his eyes had about three times more texture than the rubber appliances, so it was like trying to blend a sheet of plastic onto a bed of sand. Even after it became routine, it was still difficult to pull off. I solved the problem by using colors to give a texture to the rubber pieces, while around his eyes I used five or six colors to block his texture. The goal was to create a uniform texture all over his face that was not seen, but felt. It was a tough job.\" Perhaps the most difficult aspect of the task was the need to subject Mandy Patinkin to this grueling ordeal nearly every day of the sixteen-week shoot. As much as possible, Elek attempted to ease the actor's discomfort - though in at least one instance his efforts backfired. \"Early in the show, Mandy's baldcap was so tight that it gave him a headache, so we began stretching the baldcaps so they would slip on easier. But since the whole reason for the baldcap was to provide an anchor for the headpiece, when the baldcap was loose, so was the headFailing to impose his will on cabaret owner Joshua Strader (Jeff Kober) - whose club he sees as a vital link in the dissemination of narcotics within the alien community - entrepreneur William Harcourt (Terence Stamp) resorts to murder by having his former associate thrown bodily into seawater which is highly corrosive to alien flesh. / In a hostile ghetto bar, murder witness Trent Porter is questioned by Sykes and Francisco. Considerable pains were taken to make each alien countenance both distinctive and distinguishable - a goal successfully met with the use of near paper-thin appliances that allowed the performers' true features to read through the makeup. / One of the most difficult assignments - accomplished by John Rosengrant - was the transformation of actress Leslie Bevis into the exotic dancer Cassandra. Even under full prosthetic makeup she had to look beautiful and alluring. CINEFEX 36 • 13"],["14","Alien Nation","Exposed as a criminal and apparent self-destruction physiology and its response promptly rebom in bestial fury and altered form. John muscular hand and arm that discovers a large-scale drug manufacturing operation transmuted Harcourt was to be physically grotesque and overpowering, the makeup team had to create for the harshly exaggerated facial accentuated musculature. trapped by his pursuers, Harcourt deliberately overdoses on his own narcotic - an act of that soon proves to be anything but Though plunged into a deathlike state that deceives those unfamiliar with alien to the drug, Harcourt is Rosengrant sculpts a will be mechanized and made to dissolve on command. / Francisco secreted in an old oil refinery. / Since the character a new set of appliances and also a special bodysuit with 14 * CINEFEX 36 piece. I'll never forget one Friday when we were shooting in a tunnel downtown. Mandy was the only alien actor working and the makeup was unusable thanks to that loose baldcap! Whenever he looked up, a giant wrinkle ran across his forehead. When the person who helped me apply the makeup saw that, he walked away - but I could not. I had to face the director and the producer and everybody else who asked, 'Why is his face doing that?' I was the one who had to tell them: Sorry, guys. I can't do a thing about it because it's all one piece by now.' In order to correct the problem, I would have had to take the whole thing off and start over again. Fortunately, the moment I told them that it started to rain. I tell you, I must be the best rain dancer in the world, because I prayed for it to keep on pouring and it did! They had to call off the whole day because of weather.\" That night, as much as he hated to, Elek had to lay down the law to Patinkin. \"I explained to him that he had to understand it was nothing personal, but I could not afford to do that kind of thing anymore. From then on, you should have seen the baldcap on him - it was like a helmet. We nailed that makeup down so well that no matter what he did to it, it never loosened up.\" Although Alien Nation turned out to be a much more demanding show for both Elek and Patinkin than either had anticipated, neither of them lost their sense of humor - though there was generally little time to express it. \"I was doing Mandy's makeup for three or four weeks when his two little sons - who are five and three years old - came out to visit the set. One of the kids watched the whole makeup, and at the end he said, 'Daddy, I thought aliens were bluel' We all laughed. When Mandy was done shooting, I painted him blue.\" Laughs were few and far between for Elek, especially when he found himself having to deal with some of the less-thanperfect appliances supplied by the overworked lab crew. \"They were making headpieces twenty-four hours a day because they were so big and time-consuming to produce and because we needed a new one for each character every day. For that reason, pieces would sometimes come from the lab with bumps and air bubbles here and there. Since the lab was so overloaded, they could not always supply my crew with the best possible pieces. They had no choice - which meant we had no choice. When these defective pieces came in, my first reaction was to run to the phone and say, 'You guys really did a damn good job on me again' But then they would remind me that they only had twelve hours to make a piece that normally took fourteen hours and it had been the best they could do. I knew they were right, but I would go back to work fuming because / was the one who had to look the director in the eye and say, \"That's all we have.\" It was with no little irony that the day Alec Gillis arrived on set to play a bit part as an alien his appliance pieces did not fit. \"We got the pieces from the lab that day,\" Elek recalled, \"and we all sat down and went to work like we normally did. All of a sudden the two people who were doing Alec realized something was wrong - the two cheek pieces were about an inch-and-a-half too small. I was right in the middle of my usual makeup on Mandy, but I looked at the pieces and there was no question - they were unusable and we had no backups. Then I remembered that we were going to have a new character on the next day who was supposed to wear one of the mix-andmatch makeups and I had told the lab guys to send out some extra pieces so we could make the best fit. I said: 'Get those pieces and use them on Alec. That will give us twenty-four hours to figure out what we're going to do tomorrow.' So we sorted through the pieces umtil we found some that worked and stuck them on.\" After the fact, it was found that the original pieces had not been defectire as presumed - merely mislabeled. Gillis was not the only effects coordinator to appear in a bit part. \"Almost all of us got a chance to do a small part,\" said Tom Woodruff, \"so Alec Gillis appeared in a bar scene while John Rosengrant and Richard Landon were at one of the drug deals. I was supposed to be one of the first aliens you see - a busboy working at a bar - but my part was reshot because the executives at Fox thought I looked too much like Mandy Patinkin. Although I was a little disappointed, I really got a kick out of being in makeup again. I had not done that since I was about fourteen, and it was a real treat to have someone else put the pieces on.\" Actors rarely enjoy wearing full prosthetic makeups, but nothing prepared Elek and his crew for Terence Stamp's incredibly difficult behavior. \"When Terence came in to be made up for the first time,\" said Elek, \"he almost broke his contract because he insisted no one had informed him that his entire head would be covered by the makeup - he said he was claustrophobic.\" Having expressed his displeasure in unequivocal terms, the actor nonetheless submitted to makeup artist John Elliott whom Elek had assigned to do his application. \"I had to put John on Terence because I was busy doing Mandy every day. Since they had a lot of scenes together, I knew I would never be able to handle them both. At the start I said to John: 'Be careful with Terence. Feel him out each time he comes in. If he's in a good mood, talk to him; if he isn't, don't. Get him out of that chair and to the set as quickly as possible. Then after they shoot, clean him up right away and let him go.' And that's exactly what he did.\" To accommodate Stamp, his scenes were invariably shot as soon as he arrived on the set in makeup. \"The director wanted to shoot Terence as quickly as he could. Unfortunately, that meant that a lot of the other actors - including Mandy - had to wait around, sometimes for hours, before they got in front of the camera. Some of my actors didn't get to work at all.\" Despite the concessions, Stamp's cooperation became increasingly more difficult to obtain - much to the chagrin of Alec Gillis and John Rosengrant who were tasked with achieving Harcourt's transformation and ultimate disintegration. \"It was really sad,\" said Gillis. \"By the end of the show, Terence was so sick of the makeup that he refused to wear the transformation suit we had made specifically to fit him. As a result, we had to"],["15","Alien Nation","CINEFEX 36 • 15 FUTURE TENSE"],["16","Alien Nation","put our transformation appliances on a stuntman who looked nothing at all like Terence - he had a much thinner face. We put the pieces on him anyway, but I personally don't think the makeup looks as good as it would have if Terence Stamp had worn it.\" The Harcourt transformation occurs during the waterfront climax of Alien Nation when the drug kingpin - in a moment of defiance and desperation - swallows an apparently fatal quantity of his own narcotic. Little do the ambulance attendants realize, however - as they carry away the seemingly lifeless body - that aliens who overdose on this particular drug later transform into a faster, more ferocious and dangerous breed. When Francisco and Sykes get an emergency call, they rush out to the docks where they find the ambulance overturned, its doors ripped from their hinges and the attendants dead. The Harcourt monster is loosel Surprisingly, the actual transformation is handled entirely off-screen - a welcome respite from the overdone puffing bladders and spastic cabling that have characterized nearly every horror film since The Howling. \"Harcourt is revealed lurking in the darkness already transformed,\" Gillis remarked, \"so there is no actual scene where you see his body swelling. I think it's better that this was treated in a dramatic way - personally, I'm tired of transformations. I think there have been some excellent ones in the past, but who needs to see it anymore? We were much more interested in the final result than in watching it happen.\" Although the shooting script described the transformed Harcourt as a grotesque, oversized version of the alien design, like most of the effects in the film, this too was softened. Consequently, the makeup became about as subtle as a monster makeup can be and still be called a monster makeup. \"The way the script was written had us thinking about getting a huge actor like Kevin Peter Hall to play the part - but that was all scaled down by the time we got around to sculpting the transformation suit. We did not go for a giant Lou Ferrigno 'Hulk' - it was more subtle than that. We were somewhat limited by the fact that the transformation had to occur in the period of an hour-and-a-half or so between the time he overdoses and the time he faces Patinkin and Caan by the docks. Basically, I just exaggerated everything that was there in Stamp's normal phase makeup, only I made it more ferocious and bestial. I gave him a tumorous-looking head which implied some radical internal change. His skull was lumpier, his facial features exaggerated and his skin texture was more leathery. In my mind, he's a dead man - it's his last rush. It's as if he's become this psycho junkie whose body has gone through this transformation and all this pain, and who's probably going to die anyway.\" Harcourt finally meets a well-deserved end after a showdown with Sykes results in his total immersion in seawater - which, it has been established, dissolves alien flesh like acid. The disintegration effect was handled in two stages. Stage one involved a dissolving head and body accentuated with on-stage 16 4 CINEFEX 36"],["17","Alien Nation","effects rigged by Zoltan Elek. Stage two featured a nearly fleshless artificial arm which needed to be thrust up out of the water. The disintegration heads and bodysuits were supervised by John Rosengrant and constructed by a crew that included Grant Arndt, Greg Figiel, Mike Tric and Andy Schoneberg, \"I ended up blocking out the first stage of the disintegration as he begins to dissolve,\" Rosengrant recalled. \"It was pretty straightforward stuff - just a question of dissolving his skin to reveal bone and muscle. It was fun because we all got into doing something that was really wild and distorted.\" While the dissolving makeup had to look extreme, it also had to appear realistic within the limits of the alien anatomy Winston's crew had designed. \"We wanted to avoid the amorphous 'blob-ofblood' look you see in so many of these slasher and monster pictures. We wanted to be able to see Harcourt's bone structure rather than just the glob hanging off of it. Of course, we did use methocel slime to make it wet and nasty-looking, but the basic understructure was rooted in anatomy. We had already developed a somewhat alien anatomy for these characters - done mainly with wardrobe - to suggest that they had a different shoulder bone structure and were barrel-chested. When it came time to do the disintegration, we just used our knowledge of this alien anatomy in order to distort it so that we could make Harcourt appear to dissolve the way he really would. We used forensic medicine books to anchor the makeup in a recognizable reality, even though it is extreme and bizarre.\" Making a close-fitting appliance appear to be eaten away is one of the more difficult makeup illusions. \"On Harcourt,\" Rosengrant continued, \"the appliances were built up a lot over his cheekbones, but they became very thin on the hollow of his cheeks in order to give it a more dramatically cut and sunken look. We wanted quite a bit of his skull to be showing through, and we made it intentionally asymmetrical because injuries do not often occur symmetrically in nature. The transformation bodysuit was sculpted by Greg Figiel and Mike Tric on a cast of Terence Stamp's body - who never even wore it. Once it was molded, a spandex suit was placed over the body form and that was put in the mold while the foam rubber piece was run around it. Once the foam was cured, all the pieces meshed right into the spandex, which gave it strength, prevented it from tearing and made it very flexible.\" Because stuntman Terry Jackson - who eventually wore the suit - was working in water, several backups had to be made to allow those already immersed to dry out before they were needed again. \"The makeup really survived pretty well in the water,\" Gillis remarked. \"We sprayed it with a urethane coating which acted as a bit of a sealant, but there was really no way to effectively stop the water from leaking into the foam latex. We just had to squeeze the bodysuit out at the end of the day and dry it off. So long as we treated it carefully, we could use it over and over.\" For Harcourt's facial disintegration, the alien effects coordinators came up with a foam rubber base makeup to provide the essential structure, then superimposed a layer of gelatin appliances that served as the skin which was to dissolve via an ingenious system devised by Zoltan Elek. \"We dug out chunks from the foam rubber pieces, filled the holes with Bromo Seltzer, then laid the gelatin appliances over the whole thing and colored them so you could not see the holes below. Once the stuntman, the camera and everyone else was in position, we took a large syringe filled with hot water and injected it into PLaS each of the Bromo Seltzer pockets as the camera started rolling. As the Bromo Seltzer started to fizzle, the hot water began eating its way through the gelatin skin and his face appeared to bubble and melt.\" The effect took several hours to prepare, but since several cameras were used - one of which was highspeed - it needed to be shot only once. \"We didn't need more than about ten frames of the whole thing, but I wanted them to have a choice so they could use whichever footage came out the best. When we moved in with the syringe, we knew it was going to go very fast.\" For Zoltan Elek and the alien effects coordinators of Stan Winston Studios, Alien Nation proved to be one of the toughest films they had ever encountered. \"I think we learned a lot about how difficult makeup shows really are,\" Shane Mahan mused \"The littlest thing can cause the biggest problem - the tiniest air bubble ruined everybody's night! We figured that going from something like the alien queen in Aliens to these straight prosthetic makeups would be simple, but it was really a lot of work. I hope people will understand we did our best under the circumstances. Alien Nation was a big show, and we did it in an incredibly short amount of time, thanks to a terrific crew that really worked well together.\" Despite the difficulties, Tom Woodruff found working on Alien Nation a nostalgic opportunity to pay homage to his career inspiration. \"A number of us at the studio remember getting interested in makeup because of Planet of the Apes. It was one of the first big movies where most of the characters wore complete makeups. I personally got into makeup because of that movie, so to get the chance to do another big makeup picture like Alien Nation was really great.\" The theme was a recurring one. \"After Pumpkinhead, Aliens ded pumpinhead Men. and Invaders from Mars,\" concluded Alec Gillis, \"we really p show becar reall wanted to do a straight prosthetic makeup show because we thought it would be so much simpler than all those mechanical heads we'd been making. Of course, the job we got was enormous - and presented its own unique problems that mechanical creatures do not have. The whole assembly line process to create all the makeups we needed was a huge logistical challenge. At least it was nice to work on an effects show with some social significance.\" Perhaps Zoltan Elek best summarized the feelings of all concerned. \"At the end of any heavy show you say, 'Oh God, let's finish it and walk away!' A few years ago I said I did not want to do any more appliance makeups - and right now I feel that I have no desire to take on any more big shows like this one ever again. But at the same time I know - I feel it inside - if tomorrow I get offered another good one, I'll be on it!\" With a new look to match his disposition, Harcourt descends upon Sykes in a climactic waterfront confrontation. Although the facial pieces and foam rubber transformation suit were generated from clay prototypes sculpted over a lifecast of Terence Stamp, the veteran actor - who thoroughly disliked working in appliance makeup - refused to wear them. Consequently the final scenes were played by stuntman Terry Jackson. / With Harcourt dead and the emerging drug empire shattered, life continues in a gentler vein as Francisco attends to his partner and now friend whose daughter is about to be married. CINEFEX 36 • 17"],["18","Die Hard",""],["19","Die Hard","Exaggerated Reality article bı Adam Eisenbera"],["20","Die Hard","John McClane (Bruce Willis) among those taken hostage building. Though most of location at the new Fox Plaza Corporation. / For a scene in which a makeshift bomb is dropped down an elevator shaft, model shop crew chief Patrick McClung supervised the construction of a forced twenty-three feet tall. / Fully masonite structure reached nearly to the ceiling. / With reference, modelmaker Kent perspective shaft. / In the Boss Film parking lot, model director John McTiernan and Christmas Eve becomes hell night for detective whose estranged wife is by terrorists in a Los Angeles high-rise office Die Hard was shot on building in Century City, several key sequences required visual effects produced by Boss Film perspective miniature assembled on the effects stage, the wood-framed color enlargements for Gebo pieces together a section of the forced shop supervisor Mark Stetson, visual effects producer Richard Edlund, effects director of photography Bill Neil, effects art director Brent Boates observe final preparations for a highspeed miniature shot. 20 * CINEFEX 36 In the hit thriller, Die Hard, Bruce Willis plays New York City cop John McClane, who travels to Los Angeles to spend Christmas with his estranged wife and their kids. 'Tis the season to be jolly, and McClane gives it his best shot; but he soon finds himself trapped in a high-rise office building where his spouse and her coworkers are being held hostage by master criminal Hans Gruber and a gang of international terrorists. Avoiding capture himself, McClane quickly becomes the hostages' lone hope for survival as he launches a one-man counterattack on the terrorists - battling not only the genius of Gruber, but also the incompetence of a Los Angeles deputy police chief and At the center of Die Hard's action is the building in which it is set - the national headquarters of the fictional Nakatomi Corporation depicted on film by the new Fox Plaza building in Los Angeles. Located just behind the Twentieth Century Fox studio lot, the Fox Plaza is a high-tech marvel, a stunning thirty-four story tower overlooking most of Beverly Hills. For Die Hard, director John McTiernan and producers Lawrence Gordon and Joel Silver were given almost carte blanche use of the structure, still partially under construction inside. However, when the script called for the third floor to explode outward and the rooftop to go up in a giant fireball, the filmmakers had to turn somewhere else - to visual effects producer Richard Edlund and his team at Boss Film Corporation. \"We had blown the top off of a building before in Ghostbusters, \"Edlund explained, \"but this film required a much different approach. Ghostbusters was a comedy fantasy, an effects movie in which our work was showcased. Die Hard on the other hand is not an effects picture. It is an action film in which the effects could not look like effects at all. Our work needed to look as real as possible to blend in with the many physical effects and full-size explosions done by production effects chief AI Di Sarro. Integrated effects. At the same time, however, producer Joel Silver wanted our shots to be as dynamic as possible and exude 'exaggerated reality.' That was his motto: 'ER' - exaggerated reality. That was what we wanted here - and ultimately, I think the movie is chock full of it.\" In Die Hard, several effects sequences - designed by visual effects art director Brent Boates - embody the ER philosophy. The first comes about halfway through the film when Los Angeles police make an ill-advised assault on the building. During the attack, the police send in an armored car which the terrorists quickly destroy by launching a small missile from an upper story window. Enraged by this brutality, McClane decides to give the terrorists a taste of their own medicine. Strapping a bundle of explosives he had captured earlier onto a chair, he hurls the makeshift bomb down an elevator shaft and blows out most of the third floor. While the explosion essentially blows outward, the third floor sequence begins on the inside when McClane first pushes the chair of explosives down the elevator shaft. In reality, the chair was a small replica shot against bluescreen and the shaft a"],["21","Die Hard","CINEFEX 36 • 21"],["22","Die Hard","22 - CINEFEX 36"],["23","Die Hard","forced-perspective miniature constructed by model shop supervisor Mark Stetson and his crew. \"The shaft model was about twenty-three feet tall,\" Stetson recalled. \"The ceiling height in our stage is twenty-six-and-a-half feet and we built the model up high enough so that Bill Neil, our director of photography, could just barely put a camera above it. We forced the perspective down from half-scale at the top to about nine inches at the bottom, so the top was about four feet by six feet and the bottom was about six by nine inches. The whole thing was framed in one-by-fours and sheeted in eighth-inch masonite. Crew chief Patrick McClung and Milius Romyn led construction of the model and added miniature details like tiny lights and un in dry rock pipes. Since the inside of the shaft featured unfinished dry rock plaster, we wanted to add tiny company seals like you usually see on real sheets of dry rock. We had some small stamps around the shop, but none of those worked so Ron Gress just painted the seals on by hand.\" When the chair bomb reaches the bottom and explodes, it not only takes out most of the third floor but also sends a huge fireball rushing back up the shaft towards McClane. To create this blast in miniature, special effects foreman Thaine Morris prepared a mixture of napthalene and MP Associates yellow flame powder. \"The napthalene added black smoke and gave color to the fire,\" Morris noted. \"It also helped our explosion match the propane gas explosions Al Di Sarro used in the liveaction - and it burns at a low temperature which is important so you do not burn up the model. The model itself was made out of masonite and styrene and we shot it three times without destroying it.\" Members of the model shop found the feat almost incredible. \"The elevator shaft was a very lightweight structure,\" marveled Stetson, \"and Thaine managed to set off maybe a half dozen pyro charges in it without damaging it significantly at all. We had prepped it with fire retardant paint and used some fireproofing, but to blow out what you actually saw in the elevator shaft and to know that the model was just eighth-inch masonite - unsupported - it was just amazing.\" To film the fireball itself, Bill Neil set his camera up above the shaft and then operated it remotely. \"The fire and smoke were of a composition one would not want to breathe,\" said Neil, \"so nobody was up at the top of the shaft when it went off. Consequently we did not know what we had for sure until we saw dailies the following morning. Just watching it from below, however, I could tell by the fact that the fire went past the camera that it was probably okay. My only concern with the sequence was lighting the forced perspective. Originally we planned to light the whole elevator shaft on the stage and shoot Die Hard photographs copyright ® 1988 by Twentieth Century Fox Film Corporation. All rights reserved. Production unit still photography by Peter Sorel and Bob Isenberg. Effects unit still photography by Virgil Mirano. Special thanks to Claire Wilson, Bette Einbinder and Dale Berliner. it high-speed, but I first shot a test on the shaft with just the miniature lights that were in there and it looked so good that we changed our approach. I figured there would be no way I could get the same look by shooting it high-speed, so we decided to use a double exposure technique. We shot the elevator shaft first with the practical lights that Mark and his people had put into it and then we shot it again with the fireball - this time with the miniature lights off. In optical we combined the two passes into one.\" The elevator shaft fireball shot was among the first to incorporate the 'exaggerated reality' technique. McTiernan wanted the fireball to start slowly at the bottom of the shaft and then build up speed until it reaches the top and blows by McClane. To accomplish this, Neil and his crew adjusted the camera speed during the filming. \"The shaft was in forced perspective,\" Neil explained, \"but the fireball of course was not. If we had left the camera running at normal speed, the fireball would have appeared to move far too fast up the first twenty floors and then slow down as it got closer to the top of the model. In tests we also discovered that the brightness of the fireball died out very quickly. To counter both these problems, we decided to slow the camera down. Actually it was John McTiernan's idea and then we developed it. We had the camera on a rheostat and we ramped it down during the shot. At the beginning, when the fireball started, the camera was running at a hundred frames per second - but then we slowed it down to about twenty frames per second by the time the fireball reached the top of the shaft. This was sort of a seat-of-the-pants approach dependent upon how fast the camera responded to being turned off and how fast the fireball was coming up. Basically, when I saw the fire start I turned the camera off - so as the fireball came up, the camera was actually slowing down which made the fire appear faster and brighter.\" When the shot was later composited, the fireball still tended to dim toward the top of the shaft, so several different takes of the explosion were combined to afford additional brightness. From the elevator shaft blast, the scene cuts directly to the outside of the building as the entire third floor appears to blow out. \"This explosion the director and producers wanted to be nasty,\" explained Brent Boates. \"They wanted it to look like all the windows and moldings were being blown out by a huge boiling cloud. They wanted it big, but at the same time not so immense that you would think the building was going to fall over.\" To accomplish this task, Boss reemployed a technique first developed on Legal Eagles. For that comedy thriller, Edlund and company had created the illusion of a real Manhattan art gallery in flames by first filming a plate of the actual structure in New York City. Then the model shop built a miniature replica of the same building out of black sheet metal - without detail, but matching in shape and contour - which was rigged to be set ablaze with numerous propane gags. Photographed after dark - in high-speed by Neil Krepela - the appropriately configured flames were then composited over the original plate of With a remote control camera mounted at the top of the shaft, Bill Neil photographed a series of pyrotechnic blasts engineered by special effects foreman Thaine Morris. Because of the forced perspective, a uniform frame rate would have made the fireball appear to move too fast near the bottom of the set and not fast enough at the top. To correct the disparity, Neil employed a rheostat to alter the camera speed from one hundred frames per second to twenty frames per second as the shot progressed. / A blast rips through the third floor as the bomb detonates. / Hundreds of giant flashbulbs were wired inside the Fox Plaza and triggered simultaneously to produce an interactive lighting effect on windows and street surfaces. The blast itself was created in miniature and composited over the Fox Plaza location footage. CINEFEX 36 • 23"],["24","Die Hard","Working in plywood faced with black formica, model constructed a section of the complete with blast boxes made from welded quarterexplosive charges required for the third-floor blowout. Photographed at night to material shot on location. / hostages are herded onto the rooftop where explosives have been rigged to detonate as soon as police helicopters them arrive. To obtain the endangering cast and crew, live-action plates featuring hostages were photographed overhead. Later at a remote Palmer shot corresponding helicopter which was then shop crew members building in miniature - inch steel to contain the intensify and isolate the blasts, the resulting pyrotechnic footage - properly shaped and positioned - was then matted into the plate As the film builds to a climax, the unwitting summoned to transport desired footage without Bruce Willis and the without the aircraft location, Bill Neil and camera operator Clinton night footage of a real composited with the rooftop material. 24 - CINEFEX 36 the building. The result was an apparent conflagration raging within the gallery. Early in negotiations for Die Hard, Edlund sent the Legal Eagles reel over to Joel Silver to show the proposed technique. Silver was impressed but skeptical. \"At that point,\" Boates recalled, \"the producers were exploring the idea of actually blowing out the third floor of the real building and doing it live as opposed to Legal Eagles style. The floor in the real building was vacant anyway, so apparently they were considering what it would cost to replace all the windows. Ultimately they wound up doing it our way; but even on the night we were setting up to shoot the plate, I remember Joel Silver saying: 'Are you sure this is going to look good? There's still a chance that we can do this stuff live.' In the end, of course, I think he was very pleased with our work.\" To obtain the required location plates, Bill Neil and his crew set up five 65mm cameras during a night shoot in front of the Fox Plaza. \"The live-action crew rigged banks of large flashbulbs inside the windows and wetted down the street to create reflections that would correlate with the explosion we were going to add,\" Boates noted. \"There were also practical lights on the building and a lot of smoke coming from the armored car that was burning in the foreground. At the correct time, the flashbulbs all went off at once to suggest an explosion inside. It took hundreds and hundreds of these big, expensive, long-duration flashbulbs to do the job. We had scoured the city looking for them and had found only enough for one take, so everything had to be planned out very carefully.\" To the relief of all concerned, the shot went off without a hitch. With the live-action plate secured, Stetson set about building the 'firebox' that would represent the building in miniature. \"This was far more complicated than the one in Legal Eagles,\" Stetson offered, \"because the Fox Plaza is a modern building faced with granite and glass and any explosion inside would create reflections on the sides of the building. To account for this, we put black formica on the outside of the firebox. Die Hardalso called for an explosion rather than just boiling flames, so we could not build simply a light gauge sheet metal firebox. Instead, we had to build blast boxes out of welded quarter-inch steel plate. The finished structure was essentially plywood - but with a cutaway area for these blast boxes. All the window structure was sheeted over with black formica to get the reflections of the blast. We also added styrene windows in front of the blast box area. The styrene is quite brittle and all of it was prescored and set in very carefully.\" Filming the explosion went smoothly as expected, with Morris once again using his napthalene flame mix to create a sudden pressure rise that would blow out all the windows. The compositing phase, however, was not without its difficulties. \"The third floor explosion we thought would be a relatively simple shot,\" Edlund recalled, \"but it proved to be quite difficult in opticals. The problems arose because the first test that we did was what ultimately wound up in the film. We shot an explosion on our model as a test, and McTiernan said: 'That looks great. I love that explosion.' We shot additional takes that were more flamboyant and bright, but there was something about the character of the first one that was better and he would not budge. This created several problems for us. First, since it was a test, the shot was not in perfect lineup. Plus it had a little bit of a cant on it, so we had to make an element from it to correct the tilt. Also there was a little bit of a size discrepancy so it had to be blown up a bit.\" The other major problem with the test take was that the explosion itself had areas of transparency in it that made it difficult to pull a perfect matte. 'When you shoot an explosion against black,\" Edlund continued, \"you can only get a matte off of it if there is enough exposure. During that first take, a cloud of black smoke got in front of the explosion creating a dark area that we could not successfully pull a matte from. The front of the actual building was lit up like a basketball court. Consequently, the explosion had to be really bright so we could get a good dense matte in order not to see any transparency. We were only talking about maybe six or seven frames that were ruining the shot the explosion would blow out, that black cloud would appear and then all of a sudden you could see the building through the explosion. To remedy the problem, our editor Dennis Michelson went into our other takes and found single frames from those that we could fit into that little area. He would take a couple of frames from this take and a couple of frames from that take. Then our optical supervisor Al Cox had to take those, make mattes of them and line them up so they would fit in the appropriate spot in the frame. At the same time, he also had to blow the footage up to 65mm from high-speed 35mm shot at 250 frames per second. The whole process was just incredibly convoluted - and all for a shot that lasted maybe thirty frames at the most.\" Ultimately, adding those explosion fragments to the original test footage afforded the effect an even greater sense of exaggerated reality. \"It gave the shot a staccato feel which cut well with what was done on the main production,\" Boates noted. \"It made it look like a series of detonations going off, and the theory was that the chair bomb somehow ignited a lot of other explosions.\" Once the third floor blows, the action inside the building grows more intense as McClane continues his assault on Gruber and his team. Gruber's real intent in taking over the building is to break into the Nakatomi vault and steal more than six hundred million dollars in negotiable bonds. To gain needed time, however, he leads the police and FBI on a wild goose chase by demanding the release of numerous terrorists held in jails around the world. Then he requests rooftop helicopter transport to Los Angeles International Airport for his men and the hostages. In reality, Gruber has the roof rigged for an 'accidental' explosion designed to destroy the helicopter and kill the hostages. In the inevitable confusion, he and his men - presumed also to be killed - would be able to escape. Unfortunately, FBI field operatives have a double cross of their own"],["25","Die Hard","planned and when McClane manages to intervene, the hostages are saved but the roof goes up in flames. The sequence begins with the approach of two helicopters which fly briskly through the streets of Century City and then climb into position over the Nakatomi landing pad. The lowlevel street shots were filmed on location by the main unit, but shots of the helicopters hovering above the Fox Plaza called for a different approach. \"This was a case where we did an effects shot because we wanted to get a helicopter real close to the principal actors and the extras without putting anyone in danger,\" Boates explained. \"First we shot a plate on the roof with the actors and explosive squibs rigged to simulate machine gun fire. Then we went out to Indian Dunes and shot a real helicopter against a night sky. It was the same helicopter used for the flying shots through the streets of Century City. If this film had required a daytime shot, we would have had to do a bluescreen composite - in which case we would probably have had to use a model. But this was at night, so it really didn't matter where the helicopter was filmed - all that mattered was where it flew in the frame.\" To photograph the helicopter, Neil and camera operator Clinton Palmer stood atop scaffolding erected some ten feet off the desert floor to keep the rotor blades from kicking dust up into the air. To gain extra height for certain shots, the scaffolding was stacked on top of a grip truck. \"It was a little bit scary because we were really close to the chopper when it was flying over our heads,\" Neil recalled. \"But we had an excellent pilot - Charles A. Tamburro. He was very much in control of the ship, and he would tell us if something we were asking for was not so safe. I felt really confident in his flying. A couple of times when the situation was not quite right, he would fly on by without trying to do his maneuver.\" With only moments to spare, McClane convinces the hostages gathered on the rooftop to climb back down into the building. Then with alarm he realizes that armed agents inside the helicopter think he is one of the terrorists. In a hail of bullets McClane darts across the roof, well aware that the whole area is going to blow at any second. In a last-ditch effort to save himself, he lashes a fire hose around his chest and leaps over the side of the building just as the rooftop erupts in flame. Caught in the blast and crippled, the helicopter tumbles off the edge of the building and explodes. Unlike the third floor blowout scene, the destruction of the helicopter was planned from the start as a miniature to be photographed directly in 35mm without subsequent optical enhancement. To accomplish this, Stetson and his crew prepared two small-scale helicopter models and a miniature representing the upper stories of the Fox Plaza. \"The top of the building was constructed at an oddball scale,\" Stetson noted, \"because we were scaling it to an available helicopter kit that matched the real helicopter used during live-action. It was a Hirobo kit at 1:7.2 scale - which is weird and unconventional, but necessary for the radio control helicopter which was develCINEFEX 36 • 25"],["26","Die Hard","26 - CINEFEX 36"],["27","Die Hard","oped around an existing mechanical package. To modify the helicopter for our needs, we hired an expert radio control pilot, Larry Jolly, who was great to work with. He ended up molding the body of the chopper and built it out of lightweight woven cloth fiberglass designed for breakaway. Then we had to make some mechanical alterations. The action called for us to sever the tail boom off of the helicopter and then have the main fuselage blow. To do that we had to cut our helicopter in half right behind the engine. Then we added a free-sliding spline joint in the tail rotor flex shaft so that Thaine would be able to split the helicopter in two with a pyro charge of reasonable size. We also brightened up the running and search lights on the helicopter.\" The building miniature - scaled to the unique dimensions of the helicopter model - was seventeen feet high. Lrected on a pipe rig twelve feet above the Boss Film parking lot, the tower rose a total of twenty-nine feet into the air. When finished, the model - constructed from plywood, aluminum and plexiglass - was detailed with photo pasteups of granite texture. \"Virgil Mirano went over to the actual building site and photographed representative stills of the side of the building,\" said Stetson. \"Then we pasted those on in strips. If we were to do such a project again, I think it would be almost an even tossup between actually painting the texture on or doing the photo printing technique. The photos did give us a real nice finish on the side of the building - and it would have been maybe a little bit harder to get that consistently with paint - but on the other hand, Ron Gress is such a great painter that I am sure he could have done it. In fact, he was doing a lot of touchup on the granite texture anyway. To help match the scale, we also paid close attention to little details like the window frames and things like that.\" With the miniature completed, the focus now shifted to executing the complicated rooftop and helicopter explosions. \"We didn't want to just nuke the chopper,\" Morris explained. \"We wanted to cut it off at the tail rotor, let the blade go down in flames, have the boom pivot toward the building and then make the rest of the fuselage explode and crash to the ground - and we could have no strings attached. It was a tough order. Kelly Kirby, Kerry Owens and I rigged the chopper so that the tail boom would be separated from the fuselage by 7.5 grain primer cord, and then three small napthalene explosions would follow closely to mask the fact that it was a model. A throwaway timer was constructed to fire the devices upon being energized. When we turned on the battery, it would fire one, count five, fire another, count five and fire again. We were firing them at .004 seconds apart.\" To set off the initial explosion inside the model, a tether line was run from the helicopter to the miniature skyscraper. When the helicopter pulled far enough away from the building, the tether would pull out a contact and trigger the first blast. \"I don't like using radio control for explosives,\" Morris insisted. \"It's too chancy. This chopper was fully capable of flying - and Mistaken for one of the terrorists, McClane dodges a hail of bullets being fired at him from a helicopter matted into the scene. / Moments later - in full miniature - the rooftop erupts spectacularly. Caught in the blast and crippled, the helicopter tumbles off the side of the building and explodes. / In a uniquely achieved bluescreen composite, arch criminal Hans Gruber (Alan Rickman) plummets to his death from the thirtieth floor of the giant skyscraper. / Airbrush artist Ron Gress provides camouflage coloration for one of the helicopters used in the rooftop explosion. Modified from an existing radio control helicopter kit by operator Larry Jolly, the airworthy miniature was skinned with breakaway fiberglass and rigged to blow apart on cue. / In a daylight practice run, the helicopter hovers over a replica of the upper portion of the building. Detailed with photo enlargements of granite facings taken at the actual location site, the seventeen-foot-tall structure was rigged with pyrotechnics and then photographed at night with a high-speed camera. CINEFEX 36 • 27"],["28","Die Hard","suspending a performer on dollies away - an actual fall was devised for the death Photographed with a highspeed camera from above, actor Alan Rickman allowed himself to drop twenty-five feet onto an air bag covered with bluescreen material. His image was then isolated and matted into a live-action focus by hand was virtually developed a computerized sporting events - was hired to assist. From across the stage, Vye tracked the fall with his video camera which directly to the film camera. / Another giant miniature - representing the front face of the Fox Plaza almost in its entirety - was constructed for the last shot in the film. To avoid the artificiality inherent in bluescreen falling shots - normally accomplished by wires while the camera scene of Hans Gruber. plate taken from the thirtieth floor of the Fox Plaza. / Since following impossible, technician Richard Vye - who had follow-focus system for - via computer link - relayed the essential focusing instructions 28 * CINEFEX 36 if something went wrong and it took off, it would have been flying around with a loaded bomb in it. That's not exactly what you want in a tightly packed neighborhood like the one we have around our facility.\" In addition to the tether line, Morris also prepared a device to push the chopper up and over the edge of the rooftop. At the same time, however, Jolly would be manipulating his own set of radio controls to maneuver the helicopter - a situation which prompted a friendly rivalry between the two men. \"Thaine and Larry were joking between themselves that it was going to be a race to see whether Thaine pushed the helicopter off first or whether Larry flew it off,\" recalled Stetson. \"Thaine's sequencer not only programmed the explosive charges on top of the building but also the catapult to knock the helicopter over the edge. We were shooting at a very high frame rate and Larry's flying had to be almost instinctive because everything was happening so fast. During the first take, everyting went fine; but in the second take - which was the glory take - Thaine's device failed. Fortunately, Larry saw the explosive charge go and instinctively flew the helicopter off. This of course pulled the tether and forced the helicopter to explode. Larry saved the show and the shot turned out great.\" The explosions on top of the model building were designed to intercut well with some live-action pyrotechnics done by Al Di Sarro during the main unit shoot. \"Thaine was careful in all these instances to match Al's practical fire on location,\" noted Boates, \"not just in the look of the explosions, but in their overall volume. Al had some gigantic blasts out there. For one long shot, the camera was positioned about two miles away from the real building and Al used a huge mortar to create a gas fireball on the roof. When it went off, it rolled off the edge of the building and you could see a good part of the fireball from the ground below. Al did some things that I had not imagined could be done.\" When the roof blows, McClane avoids the brunt of the blast by swinging over the edge on the fire hose. Dangling several floors down, he rappels outward, shoots a hole in a window and swings inside to safety. With the rooftop in flames, all hell breaks loose in the building. Seeking to rescue his still-captive wife from the terrorist ringleader, McClane scours the building and finds the two together near the thirtieth floor bank vault. In the confrontation that follows, McClane shoots Gruber, propelling him backwards through a window. Falling, the terrorist clutches onto his hostage and almost pulls her out the window with him. Dangling against the side of the building, Gruber slowly brings his pistol up to fire at the lone cop who has singlehandedly foiled his near perfect scheme. With not a second to spare, McClane manages to pull his wife free and Gruber plummets to the pavement far below. Having a villain plunge to his death is a staple of the action film genre - but in this instance, McTiernan was determined to somehow do it differently. \"First of all,\" said Edlund, \"John realized that every time you hang a guy from wires in front of a bluescreen and pull a camera back, you get this sort of High Anxiety look. It worked in that case because it was a comedy - and it worked when we had Luke Skywalker falling down a tunnel in The Empire Strikes Back because he was very small in the frame. Usually, however, it has a very distinctive look that simply does not work well at all.\" What McTiernan wanted was to add more realism to the fall by showing actor Alan Rickman actually falling away from camera. To accomplish this, the actor was filmed on a stage dropping from a height of about twenty-five feet onto an air bag covered with bluescreen material. \"At the beginning of the shot, Alan was hanging very close to the camera lens. Then he let go and fell to the air pillow below while we filmed him at three hundred frames per second using an Image 300 camera. Initially, Alan had some kind of problem with his back and did not want to do the stunt. But McTiernan talked him into it and we got this fabulous look of real terror in his face when he was falling away. We could never have gotten such realism by hanging him on wires and moving the camera.\" The setup for the fall was simple enough, but keeping the actor in focus was another matter. \"The problem was that we had a shallow depth of field at the beginning of the shot,\" said Neil, \"and then towards the end we had to hold his full figure. In between these two extremes the actor would fall so quickly that it was next to impossible for an operator to try and pull focus on him. Out of a dozen tries, you might get a couple that would be pretty good - or you might not. Fortunately, Jan De Bont - the main unit director of photography - mentioned Richard Vye who had a computerized follow-focus system. We talked with Richard and it became clear that his system would be a real asset for the shot.\" Vye's unique system - developed originally to track sporting events - involved a video camera and a computer link. \"Our camera was shooting straight down, watching the actor fall away,\" Neil elaborated, \"while Richard was off to the side shooting at ninety degrees to the fall. He basically tracked the fall by pointing his video camera at the actor - in particular, focusing on his ear. He had a little motor on his lens and an encoder that would feed positions to a small Apple computer. The computer took this information and then controlled the focus on our film camera. By setting up marking positions in advance, the computer knew exactly what the focus should be at these different positions and racked the focus on our camera accordingly.\" To prepare for the shot, Vye first practiced by tracking the paths of falling tennis balls. \"After he mastered tennis balls, Richard then had to keep his cross-hairs on Alan Rickman's ear during the real shot. He was not on perfectly all of the time, but of the four takes that we did we got two that were serviceable. In perhaps the best take, Rickman's tie floated up in front of his face and ruined the shot. In the take that was ultimately selected, I think the focus holds pretty well. If we had not had that computer focus system, we would probably still be doing it.\""],["29","Die Hard","CINEFEX 36 • 29 1333-"],["30","Die Hard","30 - CINEFEX 36"],["31","Die Hard","Once the bluescreen shoot was completed, Neil went on location at the Fox Plaza to photograph a background plate out a window on the thirtieth floor. Several takes were accomplished, each with a slightly different tilt to match those made on Rickman when he was falling. This 65mm background plate was then married with the bluescreen footage by optical superviser Al Cox and his crew. \"When our composite was complete,\" con that e Edlund recalled, \"McTiernan loved the shot so much that he kept asking, 'Can't I get about four more frames?' The problem was we could only use about a fifteen-foot section of the drop because after that Alan started falling into the lights illuminating the bluescreen. Another problem we had on the shot was that since we were not using a wide-angle lens, we had to use about forty nine-lights to illuminate Alan and the bluescreen. It was just a hideous amount of light.\" The siege on the Nakatomi building ends with the death of Gruber. In the aftermath, the camera tilts from a fire still raging on the rooftop all the way down to the street level. While in the film this appears to be one continuous tilt-down, the shot really begins on a large model of the top of the building and then - in mid-tilt - dissolves to the actual building and the ground location below. \"The shot was conceived by production designer Jackson DeGovia,\" Boates recalled. \"The basic idea was that we first would do a tilt-down on the real building, do an identical tilt on a burning model and then marry the two somewhere in between. The first step then was to photograph the live-action plate. The scene on location was just enormous. There was helicopter wreckage all over the Fox Plaza and hundreds of extras were standing around watching. The camera was on a crane that was held in place by several guys to keep it steady. Bill Neil did his tilt-down, and then once he was through the crane was released and it went into a big graceful move over the entire scene in front of the building. After that we went back to Boss where our job was to create an identical tilt-down on a burning miniature and match it up to the live-action just before the crane started its move.\" Neil shot the tilt-down on the real building essentially freehand, which complicated later attempts to match the move precisely on the miniature. To help resolve the problem, Neil and Mark Harden first projected the plate photography on a wall covered in white and plotted the original tilt movements with the aid of a computer. Then they developed a motion control program that reproduced the freehand tilt on the plate. Despite careful planning, however, Neil still had to make some slight alterations due to last-minute concept changes. Likewise, Cox in opticals had to make adjustments in lineup during the compositing stage. To recreate the required section of the building, whole building except the bottom three floors. It stood twentythree feet tall out in the parking lot. Bill tracked his tilt-down and then used the program to shoot the model. Once again, Thaine had rigged a fire on the roof and we had wreckage built into the top already. It was an aftermath shot so it had to look like a large explosion had gone off.\" Model shop crew members also cut up reams of tissue paper to create miniature documents seen fluttering down from the upper floors. \"Shooting the paper was actually quite difficult,\" Edlund noted. \"It was like shooting little postage stamps on the stage against black. Bill Neil made camera moves on them and shot many takes so that we would have a lot of material to work with. The paper was all so elusive and it floated down so fast that it was impossible to plot out any lengthy moves. All Bill could go by was what looked right. So we shot a number of takes there and they all dissolve in and out. There are probably about ten different layers of composited paper in that one tiltdown. We also added paper to the wide-angle live-action scenes afterwards.\" While the effects crew originally thought they would need these scraps for a wipe between the miniature and the real building, no such wipe was needed. The two elements blended seamlessly together during the tilt-down. Besides destroying the Fox Plaza and several helicopters, Boss Film exaggerated reality in yet another way. Since the plot required McClane to be without shoes and socks for most of the film, the model shop made castings of Bruce Willis' feet and prepared latex 'barefoot' boots that he could wear over his real feet. \"He wore our rubbers just to protect him when he had to run on steel grates or around the decks covered with broken glass,\" Stetson explained. \"Joe English took impressions of his feet, brought the casts back here and then built them up a little with clay. Kent Jones did some of the sculpting work on them. We then took a mold off the buildups, cast them in latex and put dacron fabric in the bottoms to give them more strength in the soles. Dale Brady did the finishing work. I guess they were pretty comfortable. Bruce would put some talcum powder on his feet and then slip them on. Whenever there were closeups, his real feet were shown - but our boots were used for the medium shots. During the filming they were beaten up pretty badly - we made six or eight pairs and we repaired most of them at least once or twice.\" \"Die Hard was a great project for us to work on,\" concluded Edlund, \"not only because it had a terrific story, but because we had to wing the show to a degree. Several of the shots - like exploding the miniature helicopter or filming the Fox Plaza for the final tilt-down - required a looser approach than we generally have with shows that are oriented more toward motion control. One of the things I like about producer Joel Silver is that he understands that winging it is an integral part of movie making and he is prepared for changes and surprises. After we finished the helicopter crash shot and sent over the footage, Joel called me up and said: That shot is totally legitimatel Are all the shots as good as that one?' I said, 'Yeah, all the other shots are looking pretty good.' 'Good?!' he said. 'They've got to look great!' In the end, I think we did a pretty good job of fulfilling that demand.\" The final scene was envisioned as a full tiltdown from the top of the skyscraper to the wreckagestrewn concourse below. Since the rooftop had to be visibly scarred and still buming, a miniature was necessary for the first part of the shot. Bill Neil began by executing a crane shot on the real building that tilted down its full length and then swept over the adjacent street scene. After that, a twenty-three-foot-tall miniature was erected in the Boss Film parking lot, set ablaze and photographed with an identical tilt. Halfway down the building, the miniature footage was dissolved seamlessly into the live-action. Documents wafting down from the blasted upper stories were actually tiny pieces of paper photographed against black and matted over the finished composite. CINEFEX 36 • 31"],["32","Dead Ringers",""],["33","Dead Ringers","DOUBLE VISION article by Don Shay"],["34","Dead Ringers","Haley - playing nine-yearand Beverly Mantle in the prologue to Dead Ringers - are instructed on some of the finer points of female anatomy by director David preeminent gynecologists - living and working together in total symbiosis - whose shattered when a celebrated actress enters their lives. / Playing both twins as adults was actor Jeremy Irons who created subtly distinctive wardrobe and makeup. Since effects supervisor Lee Wilson was engaged to explore new applications of traditional Using diffused and moving splits coupled with motion into the twinning shots - Jonathan and Nicholas old identical twins Elliot Cronenberg. Some thirty years later they will be ordered existence is personalities for each, employing only the slightest variations in many key sequences featured the brothers in close interaction, optical split-screen effects work. control, Wilson and his associates were able to incorporate considerable freedom of movement both for the actor and for the camera. 34 - CINEFEX 36 Motion pictures had barely flickered their way into existence before cinema pioneers of vision and imagination began breaking ranks with the stagebound conventions of dramatic presentation. In examining and exploiting the characteristics peculiar to motion picture cameras, fledgling filmmakers discovered that by carefully positioning black masks to shield portions of the frame from exposure, then reversing the masks and backwinding the film, a two-pass in-camera matte could be produced that would enable an actor to appear side-by-side with himself. From this unsophisticated bit of trickery - refined somewhat with the advent of optical printing - came the impetus for such classic dual performances as Ronald Colman's royal impersonator in Prisoner of Zenda and Hayley Mills' twin teenagers in The Parent Trap. Though many of the early applications were quite startling and magical, the basic technique - used unremittingly in both film and television - has long since been reduced to a tired staple. Only this past year - with the release of two very different kinds of films - have the fundamentals of the approach been reexamined and revitalized with an influx of up-to-date technology. It was not, however, a technological imperative that drew director David Cronenberg to Dead Ringers - a dark and disturbing account of twin gynecologists bedeviled by drugs and shackled by a lifelong psychic bond that propels them inexorably toward mutual self-destruction. \"I had always been interested in the idea of twins as subject matter for a film, though not necessarily at first the reality of twins. The relationship between mind and body is a theme which has run throughout my films. We tend to think of our individuality in terms of body - how we look, how we sound, how we smell. With twins, that approach immediately comes into question. You have to look for the essence of an individual somewhere else.\" It was shortly after the release of Scanners in 1980, when Cronenberg was receiving his first major taste of recognition within the international film community, that the twins project first presented itself to him. \"I didn't have too many connections in Los Angeles at the time. I was getting an agent and a lawyer in LA. - but I didn't really know anybody, so I went down for a couple weeks just to meet people. One of the people who asked to see me was Carol Baum - who is one of the executive producers on this show. At the time she was working for Lorimar and she said that she loved Scanners and hoped there was something we could do together. She told me she had always wanted to do a remake of The Dark Mirror-which was a twins film with Olivia de Havilland - but I told her I was not really interested in that because it was a traditional twins story about a crazed killer twin and an innocent naive twin and I had already realized that most twins stories were either that or they were broad comedies. I said if I were to do a film about twins I would want it to be about two real complex human beings, neither one of which is all good or all bad. So she immediately said, Well, in that case you have to read this book.\" The book was a 1977 novel by Bari Wood and Jack Geasland called Twins. Though ostensibly a work of fiction, the psychological thriller was clearly rooted in the bizarre real-life case of identical twins Cyril and Stewart Marcus - brilliant young physicians who in 1975 were found dead in their luxury Manhattan apartment under circumstances that scandalously suggested a fatal attraction to barbiturates. Cronenberg was drawn instantly to the story. \"The idea of these crazed identical twin gynecologists was so extraordinary that I thought, Wow, I really want to do my own version of that.' I didn't want to do the book, per se, but there were elements in it that were very exciting to me.\" The novel was optioned and Cronenberg engaged screenwriter Norman Snider to develop it into a screenplay that more closely fit his personal vision of the material. \"I felt that the book, having established some very wonderful premises, sort of went off in a direction that was not very productive - at least for what I wanted, In the book, for example, one of the twins is gay and there is a scene where the two twins have sex with each other. To me that just felt wrong. If one of them is gay and one of them is not, then already they are different in a very essential way when the point of the whole story should be how similar they are. Also in the book, one of the twins gets married - another thing I thought would have separated them too much.\" Working under Cronenberg's direction, Snider produced two early drafts which lay dormant for several years until the project finally caught fire. Cronenberg - who had completed three films in the interim - then added his own personal stamp with a major rewrite and several revisions, the last of which went before the cameras in February 1988. The seven-year span from concept to completion offers only a hint at the difficulties involved in bringing the film to fruition. The very nature of the Twins project was not such that major money interests were eager to back it. Producer Marc Boyman - who had worked extensively in Canadian and American television and produced the horror feature The Incubus - joined the project in 1982 and spent the next several years trying without much success to secure the necessary funding. Cronenberg, meanwhile, went on to direct both Videodrome and The Dead Zone. Another detour occurred when Boyman learned that Twentieth Century Fox was contemplating a remake of The Fly and successfully proposed himself and Cronenberg as producer and director. Again Twins was put on the back burner. Once The Fly was completed, Dino De Laurentiis - executive producer on The Dead Zone- approached Cronenberg about the possibility of another collaboration and was sold on the Twins project. Preproduction began in Toronto in December 1986 with the procurement of a giant warehouse where clinic and apartment Dead Ringers photographs copyright © 1988 by The Mantle Clinic II, Ltd. All rights reserved. Production unit still photography by Attila Dory, Additional still photography by Richard Palin. Special thanks to Jeremy Podeswa, Bill Krohn, Sandra Tucker, Bette Einbinder and Dale Berliner."],["35","Dead Ringers","sets for the largely interior-based film were constructed under the supervision of production designer Carol Spier. By midsummer of the following year, however, De Laurentiis suffered the first of a series of financial setbacks that would eventually cost him his production company. Twins was a casualty of the times. With a million dollars worth of sets nearly completed, Boyman and Cronenberg took over the warehouse lease and rented out portions of it to meet expenses while looking for alternate financing. Ultimately, Twentieth Century fox - extremely pleased with the boxoffice performance of The Flycame to the rescue. Funding was not the only problem that plagued the production. For a long while Cronenberg was unable to find an actor of stature willing to play the dual lead. \"My first instinct was to set the film in Toronto and have either a Canadian or an American actor play the part. Toronto has a very heavy medical presence and a lot of very innovative things are done here, so it was perfectly feasible to have these celebrated fertility specialists operating out of this city. But I did have trouble casting it, as I had with The Fly. The films that I make tend to be very scary ones for actors. A lot of people's first reaction would be, 'Well, wouldn't any actor love to play twins?' In fact, though, what we found was that while some of them liked the idea at first blush, when they started to see how hard it was and how technically demanding it was going to be they began to back away. It's a very schizophrenic experience to play twins properly - and because these twins are so real, it's even more schizophrenic. Then, of course, there was the nature of the thing - the fact that it's bizarre and it's gynecologists. The current generation of American actors tends still to be in the macho street-smart mold. They don't mind playing killers and maniacs, but to play a gynecologist really scares them. We had the same problem when we were trying to hustle the film and get it going. A number of potential backers said to us: 'Well, can't they be lawyers?' And I said: 'If you think they can be lawyers, then you obviously don't get it because it's also about sexuality and it's about men and women and a lot of other things. It is of the essence that they be gynecologists.' Anyway, all these things combined with the normal problems of availability and scheduling and so on meant that we had to finally give up the idea of a North American actor and start looking elsewhere. Because the language is important to me - because my characters tend to be very articulate and witty and quick - I didn't want to consider a French actor or a German actor or an Italian actor and risk having to deal with any problems in that regard. So that meant England. And when I started thinking England, then Jeremy Irons was absolutely my first choice.\" A consummate stage actor with a handful of distinguished film credits, Jeremy Irons was indeed taken with the prospect of playing the Mantle twins. \"I was both interested and put off by the idea,\" Irons qualified. \"The idea of playing twins is quite intriguing, but it definitely has its drawbacks. I find film itself to be difficult. In film an actor has to overcome what is basically CINEFEX 36 • 35"],["36","Dead Ringers","36 - CINEFEX 36 FANAVISI NDi"],["37","Dead Ringers","a technical medium. He has to put life history and emotion and passion into a role and at the same time try to disregard the lights and cameras and guys in headphones standing around - it's a fairly unhelpful situation. When you add the challenge of playing opposite yourself - without another actor to excite you and encourage you - that makes it even harder. I looked at a few twinning films before I came out to meet with David, and I found the performances curiously dead. It was fascinating to see two Hayley Millses or two Olivia de Havillands - how do they do that, you know? — but it was not at all as involving as seeing, for instance, Bogart and Bacall on screen because the two characters were not sparking off each other. So I realized that that was both the danger and the challenge of this film - to try and create two performances that spark off each other and not get overburdened with the technical aspects of maintaining eye lines and timings and all that sort of thing.\" It was the technical aspects, however, that concerned optical effects supervisor Lee Wilson the most. A member of the Cronenberg team on both Videodrome and The Fly, Wilson was introduced to the Twins project in January 1987 as preproduction kicked into gear under the Dino De Laurentiis banner. \"David and I began by talking about the need for doing splitscreen opticals and the possibility of doing them here in Toronto. Often things of this type are farmed out to the States, but David likes to keep things within Canada whenever he can. My feeling was that it could be done here - and should - so we started shooting some tests almost immediately. Usually with a split-screen optical, the split is already in place at the beginning of the shot and it runs on through to the end. What that does is set up a fairly predictable shot that audiences by now are trained to perceive - you have one character on one side of the frame and another on the other and your split runs somewhere down the middle. For a long time that kind of approach was standard because if you tried to add or remove a split while the shot was running you often got a little kick that would tip your audience off to the fact that something was happening. What we wanted to be able to do was open the shot up by moving the split around, thus enabling the actor to be within his own little envelope of space that could be positioned and repositioned virtually anywhere within the frame. With careful choreography, that meant that two characters played by a single actor could be made to occupy the same space within a given shot - though obviously at somewhat different times. I got together with the people from Film Effects of Toronto - with whom I had worked on The Fly- and we did a few test shots where we would dissolve our splits in and out and move them around in the frame to see if we could make them undetectable. Provided there was enough time between one move and the next - we needed at least twenty-four frames to do a dissolve - the results were most successful. I was the principal subject and we did a variety of tests against various backgrounds and surfaces where we altered our splits from verticals to horizontals to totally irregular shapes and also tried to put them in unconventional areas where audiences would least expect to find them. The results were quite startling and went a long way toward erasing the perception that split-screen opticals are nothing more than two strips of film running side by side. Even though the intent was to make the twinning shots unobtrusive - David was not interested in making a special effects film - I took pleasure in anticipating the frustration of those moviegoers who would be sitting there in the audience just looking for splits. With this technique, every time they felt sure they had one figured out I would move it.\" Having proven the feasibility of dissolving splits in and out of shots - even panning them through from one side of the frame to the other - Wilson was not yet satisfied. Though wondrous in and of themselves, the shots thus obtained were still locked-off composites in an age where locked-off effects shots were decidedly out of fashion. What would truly liberate the twinning shots would be the incorporation of camera moves - nothing elaborate or flashy, no pirouetting dolly shots, just subtle types of movements that would ordinarily be employed as a matter of course if two actors were playing the twins rather than one. \"Motion control was the obvious route to go,\" Wilson continued. \"Unfortunately, most motion control systems are massive rigs that require forklifts to move them around - which is fine for an effects stage, but in a live situation with actors it is not something the cast and crew is generally comfortable with.\" Complicating matters was the fact that Cronenberg felt disinclined to commit to specific twinning shots in advance. \"David is not a big fan of storyboards. He likes to decide how he is going to shoot something when he is out moving around on the set. As a result, it was his policy to decide right on the spot where he wanted a twinning shot. He would get his actors in and they would do their rehearsal and he would spend his time with them. Then he would call us in and say, 'Let's do a twinning shot here.\"\" \"I decided that the movie would suffer if I had to arrange scenes around the twinning effect,\" Cronenberg explained. \"Dead Ringers is very heavily a dialogue picture - it is not an action picture at all - so the way the actors move through the room and the significance of what they do becomes especially important and I did not want to interfere with that. Consequently, my game plan was to choreograph each scene as if there were two separate actors and then figure out how to shoot it after that. If a twinning shot should suggest itself, then I would take advantage of it - but I would not force it. I wanted to have the shots serve the scene without necessarily drawing attention to themselves. If people starting saying, 'Wow, how did they do that?' - then that would mean the effects were taking them right out of the movie. For budgeting purposes and so on, I did try to calculate how many twinning shots I might use based on a formula of something like one twinning shot per scene when the twins are together. In reality, though, what happened was that in some sequences there are two and in others there are none.\" Essential to the twinning process was an on-set video system. Normally each scene began with Irons playing one of the brothers opposite an acting double. A live tap from the film camera fed directly into a portable console assembled and manned by video supervisor David Woods. With instant playback capability and a special effects generator, Woods was able to create a live video split in which the first half of the scene - isolated so that only Irons was visible - was combined with a live image of the actor playing his alternate role in the second. Through a monitor on the set, Cronenberg could then choreograph the second half of the shot with precision. Wilson and Woods review a twin shot on the video console. / Motion control assistant Rick Palin and chief programmer Randall Balsmeyer finalize preparations for a panning twin shot in the clinic storage room. / With its highly portable motion control rig - capable of operating on standard dolly track - the Balsmeyer and Everett team made possible a choice handful of decidedly nontraditional twinning shots incorporating pans and tilts, tracking moves and focus pulls. CINEFEX 36 • 37"],["38","Dead Ringers","accomplished at the studio, two of those not requiring photographed on location in the Toronto area. Included was a shot for the sequence in which Beverly makes a Suschitzky lines up a noneffects shot on Jeremy Irons. sometimes on camera - as in a carefully choreographed dance sequence when Elliot and his girlfriend (Heidi von Palleske) attempt to entice trois. / The most difficult of the motion control setups was a reverse tracking shot of the twins walking toward camera. To compensate for normal arm and body sway, Film Effects of Toronto had sequences that constantly shifted the split from side to were required - depending on background light levels dissolved in and out as the scene progressed. From start to finish, the shot required four separate split-screen Although most of the twinning shots were motion control were drunken spectacle of himself at an award ceremony. Shooting over the shoulder of acting double John Bayliss, cinematographer Peter / Usually an unseen presence, Bayliss was Beverly into a menage a to develop matting side. And since diffused splits of varying widths - different splits were mattes - each with an average of four dissolves. 38 - CINEFEX 36 Though not all of the twinning shots would require motion control, the ones that did were the most demanding from a technical standpoint. With Cronenberg's insistence upon maximum flexibility foremost in his mind, Wilson set out to find a portable motion control system that was both lightweight and user friendly. His quest eventually took him to New York and the small effects house of Balsmeyer and Everett where a prototype system was already in development. \"In terms of motion control it was nothing extraordinary,\" said creative director Randall Balsmeyer, \"just your basic IBM-AT clone - a Compaq 286 - and some Tondreau software. What was unique about it was that it was designed to do repeatable dolly-type moves without a dedicated track. Most motion control systems require heavy machined tracks that are pretty unwieldy on a live-action set. With our system we could do repeatable moves on standard Matthews dolly track which is available at any film rental place in the world. In order to make it as portable as possible, we first commissioned Joe Lewis at General Lift to build us a motor drive and transmission that could be bolted onto existing dollies. For the most part it worked fine - the film results were flawless - but because it was mounted outboard on the dolly it would tend to try and pull wheelies whenever we did quick accelerations. Also we noticed that on stock dollies there was a lot of slop in the way the wheels were mounted. In the end we decided that the only thing to do was sacrifice a little bit in terms of portablity and have Joe build us a special double steel dolly frame with the transmission mounted in the center.\" Even at that the system was remarkably lightweight and compact, consisting primarily of the custom dolly, a motorized pan and tilt head and a personal computer to run the motion control software. From Wilson's perspective, the system seemed an ideal compromise between capability and size. \"It was a very small unit - more like a train set than a motion control system - and it could be set up very simply in a short amount of time. It had only a few functions - just pans and tilts and dolly moves - but those were really all we needed.\" Perhaps most appealing was the fact that the system allowed for maximum continuity between the live-action and effects photography. Unlike most motion control systems which are programmed via joysticks - usually with trained effects technicians at the controls - the Balsmeyer and Everett unit was designed to be operated by the main unit crew. \"Although there was no hands-on involvement with the camera, the necessary controls were run to a console with video monitors carrying a live feed from a tap off the camera. There the director of photography had a little box with pan and tilt cranks mounted on it that he could operate himself if he wanted. Likewise, the focus puller had a little video camera pointed at the side of the lens so he could see his numbers on a separate monitor and make his adjustments with a remote dial. Even the key grip was able to control his dolly move with a remote accelerator.\" Aesthetic considerations aside, the approach was also economically sound. Instead of a large motion control crew, only Balsmeyer and assistant programmer Richard Palin would be required on the set. When principal photography finally began in February 1988, Cronenberg had ample cause to be grateful that the six-month delay had not cost him the services of Jeremy Irons. Despite the usual distractions of the filmmaking process and the difficulties inherent in building even a single performance out of sequence, Irons attacked with vigor the challenge of creating two separate but very similar characters. \"At first audiences will probably have trouble telling one from the other - much the way people do when they meet real twins - but once they get to know these characters better, the differences become apparent and they no longer seem indistinguishable. At least that was our intent. Physically the differences were very slight - just subtle changes in hair and wardrobe - but internally they are rather different. I thought of them as two halves of the same person - which in a way twins are - so in playing them I used two sides of myself. Elliot is active and confident and extroverted while Beverly tends to be more passive and withdrawn. For me it was a matter of energy. The two may be identical in appearance, but their energy comes from different places. It shows in their eyes and the way they speak, and it shows in their bearing. Oddly enough, when I was preparing for the film and during the first couple weeks of shooting, in order to keep them apart I tended to make them too different from each other. Once I had it clear in my mind, however, I was able to refine both performances so that the differences were actually very small - and at that point I felt quite confident that my Elliot and Beverly were indeed two different people but very much alike.\" Throughout the production, actor John Bayliss - a passable double in distant shots and from the rear - served as an alter ego and acting partner for Jeremy Irons in scenes requiring both of the twins to appear together. Though sometimes on camera, Bayliss was more often than not an invisible presence hidden outside the frame line or behind a split-screen matte. Working under the direction of Cronenberg, actor and double would block out the required action and rehearse each scene. Then the camera crew and effects team would be brought in to discuss possible twinning shots. For convenience and economy, it was decided that the motion control team would be on hand for only a portion of the shoot. \"We had them come up from New York for two weeks - two separate weeks,\" said Cronenberg. \"Both times were during periods when we were scheduled to do scenes that were pregnant with possibility, but even then there was nothing specific planned. I made no attempt to cram all of the twinning shots into that period. Instead I shot pretty much as I would have if they had not been there. If an opportunity arose, I would take it - but I felt strongly that it would not be wise to twist the film out of shape just to accommodate the twinning effect.\" Since occasional twinning shots were required throughout the eleven-week shoot, those scenes not accomplished while the motion control equipment was on hand were orchestrated from locked-off camera positions."],["39","Dead Ringers","CINEFEX 36 • 39"],["40","Dead Ringers","40 - CINEFEX 36 DISIAVA"],["41","Dead Ringers","In either instance, the process was essentially the same. \"We used a video system extensively,\" Wilson explained. \"From the film camera we had a video tap that ran to a console and several tape machines. After the scene was rehearsed, Jeremy and his acting double would play it out for the camera and we would record it on tape at the same time. David had a monitor on set so he could see exactly what he was getting. Then for the second half of the shot - when Jeremy was playing the other side of the scene - we would play back the first half with a hard split line so that David could block the shot live in his monitor. In other words, the recorded image from the first half of the shot would be on one side of the frame and the live feed of the second half would be on the other. That way David could determine if there was any danger of Jeremy crossing the matte line and whether the eye lines were working and so forth. Actually that was rarely a problem - Jeremy was almost never off. If there was a problem at all it was usually a technical one. Sometimes I would ask for a little more separation between the characters. I tried not to keep them so far apart that it would look unnatural, but the fact was that the video system gave us a hard split which was often deceptive. I knew that in optical we would generally need diffused splits to make the blends work - and since diffused splits eat up more of the frame than hard splits, I sometimes had to ask for a little more room.\" Monitors for the director and camera operator and a few extra cables were the only on-set manifestations of the video effects system that would prove so crucial to the production. The heart of the system - an arguably portable electronics console designed and operated by video supervisor David Woods - was positioned comfortably out of the way off the set. \"The system I built consisted of three video machines for recording and playing back, two time base correctors and a special effects nave late generator or switcher. In addition there was a wave form monitor and some other test equipment to make sure everything was working right. The video signal we dealt with came from a high-tech black-and-white video camera built specifically for video assist that essentially split off exactly what the film camera was seeing.\" Though designed primarily for the twinning shots, Cronenberg used this instant replay capability throughout the production to sustain verbal and visual continuity - both for himself and for Jeremy Irons whose dual performance made the filmmaking process even more disorienting than usual. It was during the twinning scenes, however, that the system would prove its true worth. \"When we were twinning I would record the first half of the shot clean - in other words, both Jeremy and his double would be visible. Sometimes there would be ten or twenty takes before David got one that he liked. If Jeremy happened to be playing Beverly in that part of the shot, we would then have to go back and replace the double playing Elliot with Jeremy playing Elliot. To do that, I used the time base correctors to lock the video machine in playback with the live camera.\" With the video playback unit and the live camera perfectly in sync, Woods then employed the special effects generator to create a split that replaced the unwanted portion of the frame from the first recorded take with a live feed from the set. \"The original intent,\" stated Woods, \"was to record the Beverly side and the Elliot side separately and then combine the two with a video split. But that approach would have taken several minutes per take and David always wanted to see everything instantly so we could move on - time was very expensive. So we tried the live splits and they worked. When we did the second side - whether in rehearsal or a take - I would play back the first side on the monitor and marry it together with the liveaction second side so that it looked like a single image with Jeremy Irons playing both Beverly and Elliot. At the same time I was recording that image onto my third videotape machine.\" Woods was somewhat limited by the capabilities of his special effects generator. \"It was just a basic switcher that I controlled with a joystick that slid up and down. It had horizontal and vertical capability and I could pan it around, but I had to fake it on some of the shots because on film they planned to do a lot of diagonals and curves to hide the splits as much as possible. Once in a while there would be a fixed split - which obviously was cheaper and easier - but for the most part David and Lee wanted to stay away from anything that straightforward and simple.\" Sound was another crucial factor in the process. \"Normally when we did the second half of a shot,\" said Wilson, \"the double was there also. Once in a while, if things got a little too tight, we would remove him if it looked like we might have trouble getting out of his way with the split. Jeremy, of course, preferred to have someone opposite him. It was important, however, that Jeremy react precisely to his own performance in the first half rather than to whatever the double happened to be doing in the second. To help in that regard, Bryan Day - who was the soundman on the set - built an audio playback system with a foot pedal switch that fed directly to the videotape machines and also to a tiny receiver in Jeremy's ear. The double had a headset on - listening to the same thing and lip-syncing to the Jeremy playback so Jeremy could react to him. Meanwhile, Mary Ann Simmons - David Woods' assistant - would follow the scene with a script in front of her and switch back and forth so that Jeremy's voice from the first take would go to the recorders and to the earphone. That way Jeremy could react to his own earlier performance and both voices on the videotape would be his.\" ten agen The end result - though often rough from both an audio and video standpoint - was generally sufficient to evaluate whether the shot was likely to work once the opticals were completed. Even though half of the final twinning shots were accomplished from locked-off camera positions, Wilson and Cronenberg took considerable pains to disguise the fact by avoiding wherever possible the familiar vertical split with one twin positioned on either side of the frame. In some instances, a simple reorientation of a stationary split was all that was needed to give the scene a fresh look. A diagonal split across a desk top To create a visual metaphor for the intense psychic bond between the brothers, Cronenberg incorporated into his screenplay a nightmare in which Beverly imagines himself and Elliot to be Siamese twins. Without opticals, the dream was achieved through the creation of a connective piece - constructed by special effects designer Gordon Smith and crew - that attached at the chest to Jeremy Irons and body double Graham Evans. Artist Don McLeod and Smith labored for hours to create a convincing blend around the edges. / Cronenberg lines up a shot on actor and double. With careful framing and cross-cutting between shots of Irons on alternate sides of the cord, the illusion was sustained that Beverly and Elliot were indeed connected. / Aware that his growing attachment to Claire Niveau (Genevieve Bujold) is creating an unprecedented rift between himself and his twin, Beverly subconsciously envisions the actress biting through the physical bond that ties him and his brother together. CINEFEX 36 • 41"],["42","Dead Ringers","detoxification program he had established for his drug dependent brother, Elliot psychically and physically. Before long they are both aimlessly through the clinic babbling incoherently of plans to reverse their selfdestructive course. A motion sequence had the camera dollying down the hallway three successive doorways. metronome was employed on the set. In optical, seven sequenced through the shot - each panning across the frame and each requiring multiple dissolves from one split width to another. / In a subsequent motion control shot, the camera dollies in slightly on the stuporous twins as they engage in a bizarre birthday celebration. To compensate for a slight during the move, the split had to be panned slowly as Unable to enforce the attempts to synchronize himself with Beverly addicted - wandering control shot for the as the twins parade past To keep the separately photographed brothers perfectly in step, a different mattes were change in camera angle the shot progressed. 42 * CINEFEX 36 in one scene enabled both brothers to appear as though they were occupying the same vertical plane in the shot. In another, a horizontal split allowed Beverly to be seen stretched out on a sofa while Elliot and his girlfriend dance quietly in the background. Panning the split was even more effective. When a drunken Beverly attempts to upstage his brother at an award ceremony, panning splits enabled the scene to begin with Elliot at the podium unwisely stepping aside to allow his brother access to the microphone. At the conclusion of the fiasco, both exit on the same side of the frame. It was the motion control shots, however, that really sold the twinning effects. When the Balsmeyer and Everett team arrived for their first week on the set in early March, expectations and anxieties were running high. As before, Cronenberg insisted on a clear rationale for each shot - avoiding the temptation to show off with the technology. \"Frankly I would have been inclined to do more,\" said Wilson, \"just because I would have loved to have seen a lot of twin shots in the film - but that that vern was not what this story was about and David kept that very much in focus. Originally - according to the way I read the script - there were about thirty-five possible twin shots in the film. But David saw it much more conservatively. In the end, he shot only about nineteen or twenty - and some of those even did not make it into the final cut.\" The motion control system quickly demonstrated that it could hold its own amidst the controlled chaos of a live-action shoot. \"Most of our setups took only a half-hour or forty-five minutes,\" recalled Balsmeyer, \"maybe a little longer on the elaborate tracking shots where the grips had to secure our dolly tracks to the floor. Even at that we were usually ready before the lighting. We had only one real embarrassment on the film and that was our very first day on the set when we were doing a shot of Elliot entering the clinic with a box of food and supplies. We had everything set up and ready to go when suddenly the servo amplifier just shorted itself out and died on the dolly. It turned out to be an ungrounded power line. So with everybody standing around watching, we had to go in and replace the servo with a stepper motor just to get the shot done. About an hour later we were up and running again.\" Even with the system debugged, cinematographer Peter Suschitzky was not an instant convert. \"Peter tends not to be too technically oriented. Mostly what concerns him is the lighting and how things look — so he was a bit wary at first. By the second day, however, he was getting pretty comfortable with us. Once he realized that we could customize the feel of things for him - electronically dampen the pan to smooth out his motions or electronically adjust the gear ratios to make fast pans simpler Though the basic approach was unchanged, the motion control shots added a few extra wrinkles to the task of generating the video split-screens needed to choreograph and then evaluate the twinning shots. \"In recording his sound,\" said Woods, \"Bryan Day used a tape recorder that produced an external time code edit which I could record. In turn, the system I built allowed me to burn those time code numbers into the corner of my video frame. After the first half of each shot was slated, the motion control operators put a cue light up in the frame and flashed it to signal the start of their move. When it came time to do the second half of the shot I would go back over the video frame by frame and give them the exact time code number when the light flashed. Since the motion control system had also recorded the time code, the computer could then be told to repeat its move when it got to that particular number. Thus, when Bryan played back his audio for Jeremy to hear, the time code was also playing back, feeding my deck so that I could match the two shots and telling the motion control unit to begin its second pass.\" One of the reasons the video console was positioned well away from the set was because the time base correctors needed to interlock the video playback unit and the film camera required cooling fans that tended to be noisy. That noise was completely overwhelmed, however, when the motion control equipment arrived. Since motion control is rarely employed in a live-action environment no sound baffling devices had been incorporated into the system. \"For an actor,\" said Irons, \"the motion control camera is really a test of one's concentration and patience. Just doing the part alone was difficult enough. After getting everything set on one side, I would then have to go around to the other side and watch a lot of things. I would have to keep the distances absolutely right - not too close, not too far - and I would have to keep the eyelines right, which were always moving. At the same time I would have to not be put off by this very noisy camera which was chugging along doing its own thing.\" Curiously it was the pan and tilt head - not the dolly - that created the principal distraction. \"The servo motor that ran the dolly was actually very quiet,\" stated Balsmeyer. \"It was the stepper motors on the pan and tilt head that made all the noise - kind of a whirring sound whenever they were running. Early on we discussed the possibility of replacing them with servos, but the production people assumed that they would be looping a great deal of the film anyway and decided that they would rather put their money into more days of noisy shooting than have a perfect system for fewer days.\" Among the most striking of the dozen or so motion control scenes is a tracking shot inside a hospital cafeteria as Elliot and Beverly enter and walk fifteen or twenty paces forward - practically elbow to elbow - while engaged in conversation. In another tracking shot inside the clinic, the brothers - stripped to suitcoats and shorts and stoned on drugs - pass in tandem through a succession of three doorways while babbling nonsensically back and forth. Both shots were very time-consuming - the first requiring a full day of takes and retakes. \"My personal favorites are actually some of the simpler looking shots,\" noted Wilson. \"As soon as I was told that we could rack focus with the motion control camera I started thinking we should"],["43","Dead Ringers","try doing it across the split line. If it worked, it seemed to me that it would be a really convincing way of blending the two sides together. We decided to try it in the sequence where the brothers are waiting to hear what the medical board plans to do after Beverly goes berserk in the operating room. Beverly is rocking himself on a day bed while Elliot is on the phone. We did a slight pan from Beverly in the background to Elliot in full closeup and pulled focus at the same time - and the shot came out wonderfully. Another of my favorites is in the clinic storage room when Elliot first discovers that Beverly is over the edge and hallucinating about mutant women. The scene is played in closeup - very tense and very dramatic - and the camera pans from Elliot to Beverly and then back again. Conceptually it was quite simple - and perhaps all the more effective for just that reason. If we had been dealing with two actors instead of just one, no one would have even thought twice about it.\" True to form and intent, Cronenberg worked out his twinning shots on set even when the motion control unit was involved. One shot, however - the final twin shot in the film - did require some preplanning, Elliot - benignly butchered by his brother - lies propped against a wall in one of the clinic examination rooms. Draped across his legs - also dead - is Beverly. \"For the death scene,\" said Wilson, \"we had to split Jeremy in half. Since there is a sheet partially covering Elliot, we took that sheet and molded it to stay rigid. The bulk of it was actually loose-fitting, but the area around his chest was molded and bolted firmly to the set. For the first part of the shot, we put a body double under the sheet and had Jeremy as Beverly draped over his lap in the fetal position. Then we removed the body double entirely - he was no longer needed since we already had the bottom half of the frame - and then we had Jeremy as Elliot slide into position under the sheet so we could film the top half. We did two alternate versions. One was a long shot - stationary - and the other was a shot that began tight on Elliot at the top and then tilted down to Beverly on the bottom. Later, in the optical phase, we were able to rotoscope a matte off the stationary portion of the sheet and just pull it through the frame during the tilt.\" Although the on-set video was an invaluable tool, it offered only a rough approximation of how the twinning shots would eventually look. Since final composites were still many months away, an intermediate step was deemed essential. \"After each twinning shot, David would see the elements in rushes and decide which ones he wanted to use. On the set he would generally determine which first half he wanted so that the second half could be matched to it - although sometimes he would pick two. We might start out with two Beverly sides and then do eight or nine Elliots to one Beverly and three or four Elliots to the other. So there were often several combinations. Once David made his final choices, the elements would go to the editors where everything was lined up and put in sync. From there the footage went to Film Effects where John Furniotis - CINEFEX 36 • 43"],["44","Dead Ringers","44 - CINEFEX 36"],["45","Dead Ringers","who was heading up the project there - did a 'color slash' or precomposite. Almost as much work would go into each slash as into a final composite in that the whole thing was rotoscoped out and mattes were generated - but everything was done very quickly. The end result was a hard-edge split that actually told us very little about how the optical was going to work because in most cases we were going to use diffused edges which would take up a lot more room. It also told us nothing about registration because we were getting all kinds of print weave from the projection - which was pretty scary at first. What it did do however was show us whether the eye lines were right and how the performance was going and generally whether or not the scene was working.\" Once the color slash passed inspection, the elements were set aside until principal photography wrapped. \"We started early on a couple of the more difficult shots, but for the most part we decided to wait and see exactly what and how much David planned to use once he got into cutting the film.\" Though no less disturbing than earlier films in the Cronenberg canon, Dead Ringers is clearly the least horrific in terms of visual imagery. Only in one fleeting nightmare sequence does the director step sideways into the queasy world of aberrant biology that over the years has become his personal stomping ground. Originally, however, there were to have been two dream sequences - both triggered by the fact that Beverly has fallen in love with actress Claire Niveau and in the process disrupted the psychic bond between himself and his brother. In the first, Beverly and Claire are in bed about to make love while Elliot - now a Siamese twin - lies next to them watching. Reacting to her lover's distress, Claire leans over and severs the connective cord with her teeth. In the second nightmare, Beverly is once again in bed with Claire when their lovemaking is disrupted by a parasitic twin whose shrunken upper body protrudes grotesquely from his chest. Protesting that he is the real Beverly, the tiny horror presses down on his host's hips for leverage and with a mighty effort rips himself free. \"Our dream life is important to us,\" noted Cronenberg. \"I'm not a mystic about it, but in a very down-to-earth practical way I think a lot of creative energy is generated when we dream and when we sleep and sometimes we are not even aware of the influence it has on our waking life. So dreams are important for my characters - and have been in most of my movies. When I was working on the script for Dead Ringers, one of the things I knew I had to do was come up with a very strong visual metaphor for the kind of incredible relationship these twins have - the fact that they are so close to each other in a positive sense and yet in a negative sense cannot escape from each other. When I came up with the dream sequences - two dreams that dealt with twins physically attached - everything suddenly crystallized for me. I knew then that the script would work. And I felt that the audience would understand from those dreams - more than from all of the talking and the switching back and forth and so on - what the inner emotional life of these twins nor of ener love thing there to eart must be like.\" Prosthetic makeup effects would be needed to achieve the dream imagery - familiar turf for Cronenberg whose prior excursions into visceral horror had employed such masters of the craft as Dick Smith, Rick Baker and Chris Walas. Walas - whose contributions to The Fly had been the most recent and most involving - was approached to do the much more limited Dead Ringers work and expressed some preliminary interest. By the time production got under way, however, Walas was otherwise engaged - as director of The Fly II. A strong supporter of Canadian talent, Cronenberg next turned to makeup designer Gordon Smith who in recent years had emerged as an artist of note - having created convincing open-heart surgery effects for Threshold and equally convincing battlefield makeups for both Salvador and Platoon, Working out of a small Toronto studio, Smith and a crew of four long-time associates spent two months fabricating a quarter-scale parasitic twin and a connective cord for the Siamese twins. Although the possibility existed, no split-screen opticals were employed in the Siamese twin dream. Instead Jeremy Irons was photographed on either side of the connective piece - opposite body double Graham Evans - and the scene was then pieced together with carefully chosen angles and selective crosscutting. \"The connection was based on an actual casting taken from the original Siamese twins,\" Smith related, \"except that it was about three times bigger. Chang and Eng were joined together at the chest facing each other. Beverly and Elliot are joined in the same general location. The connecting piece we made had a circumference of about six inches - though it was not perfectly cylindrical - and was approximately eight inches long. It could stretch another fifty percent, however, so that allowed the actors to move as far as twelve to fifteen inches away from each other. Neither twin has a belly button - but they share one between them so we had to devise something that looked like umbilical cords from each twin that extended out and joined together in this mass of tissue and pulsing veins.\" Scaling up the Chang and Eng casting and adding his own embellishments in clay, Smith produced a sculpture of the connective piece which was subsequently cast in Skinflex - a flexible urethane - by lab supervisor Derek Howard and assistant Bill Francis. Artist Don Mcleod then painted it in translucent skin tones. Though the inner workings were scarcely visible - even when bitten into - pains were taken to give the cartilaginous piece an organic looking core. \"We filled condoms with several different colors of Skinflex in liquid form, then packed them in layers on top of each other and manipulated them so that the colors would blend realistically. It looked very much like real tissue. We also made bladders out of condoms so that we could get a pulsing action as if there were blood flowing within the piece. Each bladder had its own bellows and was controlled independently so that a pulsing movement could be started on one side and progress to the other. Luckily the twins were Apparent chaos reigns on the apartment set as crew members prepare for a motion control dolly shot - later cut from the film - in which Beverly descends into the living room to recount the details of his prophetically disturbing nightmare to Elliot. / Peter Suschitzky studies a video monitor while Randy Balsmeyer plays back a motion control move. A unique feature of the Balsmeyer and Everett system was that the camera operator, focus puller and key grip - instead of relinquishing their positions to effects personnel during the motion control process - could continue to perform their normal functions electronically from a control console. / At Film Effects, project director John Furniotis and Lee Wilson check optical lineup on one of their split-screen mattes. Four months of intensive postproduction would be required to complete the pivotal twinning shots. CINEFEX 36 • 45"],["46","Dead Ringers","necessary for the siblings to appear as though they were physically touching. For the final sequence in which a roaming camera comes to rest on the twins rejoined in death, Cronenberg wanted Beverly to be found draped Crucial to the effect was a Elliot. Prior to setting up the shot, the sheet was attached and bolted to the floor and wall. For the first half of the positioned under the sheet Beverly - stretched over his Irons - as Elliot - replaced the body double. Since the preceding shot - a tilt-down from Elliot to Beverly in the connected to the final shot with a dissolve. To avoid an extra duping step - and the consequent loss of image composited the two shots as one with a built-in dissolve between them. Twelve hours of printer time were required In only one of the splitscreen shots was it across his brother's lap. sheet partially covering to a rigid fiberglass shell shot, Graham Evans was with Jeremy Irons - as legs. For the second half, placement of the sheet remained constant throughout, an irregular split could be generated along its upper edge. The same positions - was quality - Film Effects to complete the scene. 46 CINEFEX 36 positioned in such a way that we could easily hide our cables. One twin was lying on his back and the other was stretched out on his side, so we ran the six cables we needed out the back side of the connection and down through the bedding.\" On the designated shooting day, the connective piece was affixed to the actors with ordinary prosthetic adhesive. Evans was the first to undergo the ordeal; then Irons was brought in and attached to the opposite end. Since the piece connected each actor at a point just below the diaphragm - where there were no shadows or folds of skin in which to hide the seams - the blending task was an extremely arduous one that took hours to complete satisfactorily. Additional on-set time was consumed preparing for the moment in which actress Genevieve Bujold had to bite into the urethane lifeline. \"It would have been impossible for Genevieve or anyone else to bite into the piece,\" stated Smith. \"Skinflex just will not pierce like that. So we had to cut out the bite area and then seal it up with mortician's wax and a little bit of plastic. When she bit into it, she was to pull back out with a section of umbilical cord in her teeth. Surprisingly - considering his reputation - David was insistent that the scene not be too bloody. There had to be some blood, of course, and some methycellulose slime - and those two things we injected just moments before the shooting since both are water-based and tend to mix together very quickly.\" In the end, a grueling fourteen-hour day was required to complete both halves of the eerie dream sequence. Preparations for the second dream were even more complex. Cronenberg had been quite specific in his expectations - the parasitic twin had to look exactly like Jeremy Irons if Jeremy irons were a shriveled old man. \"In real life,\" said Smith, \"a parasitic twin is one that is only partially formed and generally protrudes out of the abdomen of the full twin. It ages much more quickly than the host twin and generally dies first. Once the parasitic twin dies, the host usually does also. We looked at quite a few photographs of such twins and found that most of them are very bizarrely disfigured.\" Perhaps the most unusual aspect of the assignment was the manner in which Smith and company elected to create their quarter-scale likeness of Irons. \"It seemed an impossible task to create an exact but aged version of Jeremy in quarter-scale. I knew that my hands were incapable of sculpting it. So we began by doing separate lifecasts of Jeremy's head, his torso and his arms. Then we made positives and negatives of each piece in alginate - a quickcasting material that shrinks tremendously as it dries. Normally that is not a desirable property, but in this case we decided to let the castings dry fully - either in air or in an oven - and we found that they shrank to about fifty percent of their original size. It was hard not to panic during the process because the forms did not shrink evenly at all; but by the end, they normally became proportional again. Even so, there were major distortions - some of which were appropriate, some of which were not. There was just no way to control it. We ended up doing quite a few different pieces before we got some that were usable, but the results were spectacular - every single pore was there. There was no way we could have sculpted that and made it as clean and uniform.\" After a suitable half-scale replica was obtained, the full process was repeated once again to take the figure down another fifty percent. At that point, the various body parts were assembled and desired alterations made by soaking the aliginate to render it malleable and by adding areas of sculpted clay where necessary. Finally a urethane and fiberglass mold was generated and from that a casting was made - again in Skinflex. Since the puppet was too small for a conventional fiberglass skeleton, Smith constructed one out of dental acrylic which not only was stronger than fiberglass but could also be machined. Meanwhile, mechanical designer Walter Klassen was at work on the actuating mechanisms. \"Gordon wanted the puppet to do everything.\" Klassen stated, \"so I knew from the beginning that it would have to have every possible feature in it. We were not dealing with a monster here. Everything had to work like it would on a real human being. Initially we discussed the possibility of using rods throughout the body, but we discarded that idea because we would have had to cheat the camera angles all the time to hide them. We ended up using a system of springs actuated by cables - which I prefer to the more commonly used push-pull systems. A central spring acted as the lower spine of the puppet and on top of that I built a turn mechanism for the chest activated by a series of universal joints. A second spring on top of the rib cage acted as the neck and another moved the shoulders. For the elbows, I took an old dental drill apart and used some of the mechanisms. There were a lot of pulleys and bearings in the arms to ease tension. The fingers were difficult because they were so tiny - in some cases only an eighth-of-an-inch thick - but each one could move individually.\" When the mechanics were completed and installed within the puppet, the parasitic twin was glued into a urethane replica of Jeremy Irons' torso and positioned on a cutaway bed. Irons himself - in now-standard fashion - lay on a slant board concealed beneath the bed with his head and arms carefully blended into the false torso. Although the parasitic twin was a mere fifteen inches tall, seven puppeteers were required to operate the nearly one hundred cables needed to articulate its many functions - which included lip-syncing several lines of dialogue. Though the dream sequences had been pivotal to him at the outset, Cronenberg eventually came to question their inclusion as the otherwise nonfantastical production approached final cut. To test audience reaction, the film was previewed successively with both dreams intact, one dream only and no dreams at all. Eventually Cronenberg elected to retain only the Siamese twin sequence. \"Although it was not apparent until I put it all together and screened it, the parasitic twin dream was just too far removed from the rest of the movie - it seemed almost as though it were from some other picture. I was concerned that the same might be true of the Siamese twin dream; but when"],["47","Dead Ringers","CINEFEX 36 • 47"],["48","Dead Ringers","I screened the film without it, I felt rather strongly that something was missing, Curiously though, there is a lot of dialogue in the script about Chang and Eng and the original Siamese twins - none of which I wrote or even thought about until the dream sequence sparked it. So even if I had removed the dream, its influence would still have been felt since it was quite crucial that the brothers think of themselves as Siamese twins - at first figuratively and then literally as the movie progresses.\" Principal photography was completed in late April, and soon thereafter elements for the split-screen twinning shots began filtering into Film Effects. Almost none of the work required to produce the preliminary color slashes was applicable to the finished composite phase. Everything had to be done again from scratch. \"As each twin shot was edited into the picture,\" said Wilson, \"John Furniotis and I would first decide where the split needed to go and then map out a course for it as the scene progressed. In the static shots, all we had to do was adjust for the characters' movements within the frame - though that was by no means simple since we wanted them to be able to move around as much as possible and not be limited. In the motion control shots - since the characters and the camera were moving - it was somewhat more difficult because we had to keep adjusting the split line continuously. Sometimes we would pan the split across, though more often than not we would dissolve it in and out and step it across the frame as the camera moved. Once the shot was mapped out, hi-con holdout mattes for the non-Jeremy sides were created and then the two Jeremy sides were sandwiched together. From a purely technical standpoint there was nothing terribly innovative being done in the compositing stage, although John and his people did come up with some nice refinements.\" Most significant among the refinements was the employment of two interpositives to reduce grain accumulation during optical duping. \"Grain was a major concern,\" explained John Furniotis, \"primarily because we were using standard 35mm as opposed to a larger format such as 65mm or Vistavision. Usually the way a dupe ends up looking on the screen depends a lot on the original photography. For this reason we went through a number of tests with Peter Suschitzky to determine what exposures should be used on the set. In so doing, we discovered that the best way to keep our shots looking as much like original photography as possible was to overexpose them by a stop-and-a-half or so. Even then, a couple of our early shots had a considerable amount of grain in them. So we decided to try two interpositives on one shot to see what would happen - basically do two runs at fifty percent exposure each to build up to a full normal exposure. Of course the grain from the original negative was still there, but we found that by using two interpositives we were able to greatly reduce the grain caused by the interpositives themselves. Consequently we continued doing that throughout the film.\" As anticipated, the greatest effort went into concealing the matte lines. \"The trick was to diffuse the splits,\" said Wilson, 48 - CINEFEX 36"],["49","Dead Ringers","\"but make the diffused area as narrow as possible to keep them from eating up too much of the frame. If they became too narrow, however, they would essentially become a hard-edge split - and hard-edge splits are very difficult to conceal unless you can run them up the side of something in the shot. Of course, that's where everyone expects to find them, so we almost never did that. The only vertical split in the film was a shot in the clinic where Beverly is lying on the day bed talking with Elliot. The edge of the wall between them was such a perfect place for the split that we had to use it - though the thought crossed my mind to put it somewhere else just so we could say that we had.\" Minor anomalies that went unnoticed during the live-action shoot had to be dealt with during postproduction. For a scene of the stuperous twins sitting on their day beds engaging in a bizarre birthday celebration, a door frame had to be removed in mid-shot to allow passage of the motion control camera dolly. Only in postproduction was it noticed that in the process some of the shadows already recorded in the first half of the shot were altered. In another sequence - the award ceremony disrupted by Beverly - daylight shining in through windows in the rear shifted just enough during Jeremy Irons' makeup and wardrobe change to create reflections off the stainless steel podium which had not been present during the first half of the shot. In these and other instances, corrective action was taken by creating a panoply of split-screen mattes - often in decidedly nontraditional jigsaw puzzle configurations. \"There was a lot of trial and error involved,\" said Furniotis, \"and a lot of testing. On most of the shots, not only were we moving the splits around as the scene progressed, but we were also varying them - width-wise - from one moment to the next depending on the background. Generally speaking, we would need a wider split in front of a light area than we would in front of a dark area. Focus was also a factor. On the pull focus shot between Beverly and Elliot - when Elliot is talking on the phone - we had to enlarge our split during the pan to keep it from becoming visible when the Beverly side of the frame went soft. Sometimes we had to compromise. On the junkie shuffle' scene - where the twins are walking past the clinic doorways - we ran into a situation where the split that worked on the lower half of the frame did not work well on the upper half and vice versa. In that case we had to just go with what we thought looked best under the circumstances.\" The cafeteria shot - which had been the most troublesome and time-consuming to photograph - was likewise the most troublesome and time-consuming to composite. \"Since the two characters are quite close together and walking toward the camera,\" explained Wilson, \"our biggest difficulty was keeping the matte line out of their way. When we shot it, Jeremy's acting double was not as close to the center of the frame as Jeremy was, but the normal side-to-side motion as they walked had us constantly shifting the split line - first to one side to get out of Beverly's way and then to the other side to get out of Elliot's. We also had a problem at the very beginning of the shot. Elliot comes in through the doorway and Beverly follows him a moment later. Obviously we had to slide the split over to make that work - but Elliot was wearing a dark suit and we found that if we moved the split line over too far to accommodate Beverly we would get a ghosting effect on Elliot's shoulder since the diffused split tended to make things transparent. It was very hard to tell. We would do ten or twenty key frames to check out possible problem areas, but not until we ran the shot all the way through would some of these little things become apparent. So we went through numerous takes - especially on ко тераї that shot - to get everything right. One of the things I like most about the cafeteria shot was an extra that David had walking by on Elliot's side of the frame. Elliot says hello to her and Beverly glances over as she passes. It was a subtle touch, but it really helped tie the two halves together.\" As postproduction progressed, the funding delay that had stalled principal photography for six months continued to have noticeable, if nonlethal, impact on the project. In June, Touchstone Pictures released Big Business with Bette Midler and Lily Tomlin as two sets of identical twins. Though broadly comedic and about as far removed from the Cronenberg project as imaginable, the film was nonetheless a disappointment for the effects crew as it one-upped them in introducing twinning shots made mobile via motion control. Meanwhile a second Twins project - a noneffects comedy starring Arnold Schwarzenegger and Danny DeVito as twin brothers separated at birth - was announced and went into production. After considerable negotiation, Cronenberg relinquished the Twins title under which his film had been shot - ironically to director Ivan Reitman who had coproduced his first two low-budget features. After four months of intense postproduction, the last of the twinning shots was delivered only days before the film was to have its world premiere at the Toronto Film Festival. Widely heralded as David Cronenberg's most mature film to date - and with lavish praise for Jeremy Irons in his dual role - Dead Ringers was nonetheless received with little or no notice of its flawless optical effects. The reaction was precisely what Lee Wilson had hoped for. \"I'm very happy with the twinning shots and I think they add something very special to the film - but they are subtle. What really makes them work is the performance. Jeremy is just incredible. One scene in particular comes to mind. Beverly and Elliot are eating pizza and talking over the table in their apartment - and the interaction is so precise and the eye lines are working so well that it's almost impossible not to believe there are two people sitting there. To be honest, I think what would make me happiest is if I were in a theater audience and I heard somebody say: 'Yeah, they got somebody who looks incredibly like Jeremy Irons, but I can tell the difference - they don't look that much alike.' I can almost see that happening. Jeremy is so convincing in his changes from Beverly to Elliot - using just little bits of subtle behavior - that really, at times, they don't look that much alike. In essence, Jeremy provided the effects - all we did was connect them.\" Ultimately deleted from the film was a second dream - another in which Beverly experiences the terror of being separated from a physically attached twin, In this instance, however, he finds himself host to an angry parasitic twin determined to be free. / To create a withered replica of Jeremy Irons, Gordon Smith and his crew made castings of the actor in alginate - a material which shrinks to about half its original size when fully dried - and then repeated the process a second time to reduce it still further. A skeleton was fashioned out of dental acrylic, and a complex actuating system was devised employing springs and cables. Finally the puppet was fitted into a fake torso while Irons lay on a slant board with only his head and arms exposed. Don Mcleod and Smith apply makeup to feather the blend. Stationed under the elevated set were seven cable operators assigned to articulate the puppet. CINEFEX 36 • 49"],["50","The Blob","THE RIGHT BLOB FOR THE RIGHT JOB"],["51","The Blob","Perhaps reflecting a national paranoia regarding technology and science brought on by the dawn of the nuclear age, a very particular kind of movie became popular during the 1950s. That decade produced a plethora of low-budget horror films so similar in plot and theme that only their horrific antagonists made them distinguishable from one another. Despite the fact that The Blob is now remembered primarily as the vehicle that propelled Steve McQueen into stardom, the decidedly 'B' grade film has maintained an affectionate following through the years. Containing the basic element of a classic horror story - humanity's encounter with the very epitome of evil - The Blob has been deemed worthy of a 1988 remake directed by Chuck Russell. Working with producer Jack H. Harris - who also produced the original Blob - Russell and cowriter Frank Darabont strove to retain the classic elements of the original while updating the story so that it would be credible to a more sophisticated audience. \"What's great about the Blob,\" Russell \"is that it's archetypal - a primal evil. Even commented, though the effects in the original were sometimes pretty silly, it contained the real juice of horror films - which is the catharsis that comes from facing your worst fears. The Blob is the ultimate monster, the ultimate evil. I wanted to retain that, portraying the Blob as a relentless and effective predator - something the audience could relate to with a mixture of fear and fascination. It was not our intent to stay strictly within the storyline of the original; but since the audience was bound to have certain expectations, we did try to keep its spirit. We wrote a more contemporary story and stepped up the action times ten, but we tried to remain true to the broad events of the original Blob.\" As in the original, the Blob crashes to earth from outer space. The moder twist, however, is that rather than an extraterrestrial entity, the Blob is eventually revealed to be the result of a military experiment in biological warfare gone terribly awry. In a ball of fire, it lands on the outskirts of a small ski resort town where - blood being its staple diet - it ingests one victim after another, growing ever larger as it does so. The character played by Steve McQueen in the original has been split into high school football hero Paul Taylor (Donovan Leitch) and rebel biker Brian Flagg (Kevin Dillon). With cheerleader Meg Penny (Shawnee Smith) at their side, the teenagers soon disbeast fal an di cover that all traditional attempts to kill the beast fail and - even more alarming - there are government agents involved that do not want it killed. Among the key scenes from the original that have been retained - though updated - are the The Blob photographs copyright © 1988 by Tri-Star Pictures, Inc. All rights reserved. Production unit still photography by Marsha Blackbum. Effects unit still photography by Barry Wiggins and Brian Adams. Additional photographs provided by Tony Gardner. Special thanks to Steven Fisher, Dana Schomstein and Rachel Horovitz. discovery of the Blob by an old man whose hand becomes engulfed, a classic encounter in which the gelatinous mass attacks the patrons of a movie theater and a climactic confrontation in which the Blob finally succumbs to cold. With diverse credits ranging from producer on Back to School to cowriter and director of Nightmare on Elm Street III, Chuck Russell clearly has strong ties to both comedy and horror and the intermingling of the two. But despite the cult film status of the original Blob, he was determined to resist the temptation to turn his remake into pure camp. \"I'm a thrill junkie with a sense of humor,\" Russell proclaimed. \"There is a sense of humor in The Blob, but mostly I wanted it to be thrilling - a film with some action in it. At the same time, I wanted it to embody something magical that almost goes back to our genes - to the primal universal evil. Any fantasy film requires a commitment from the filmmakers to believe in it and to give the audience enough to believe in too. If you don't play down to an audience - if your protagonists are smart enough not to go down into the basement without a light when they hear a noise - then the audience can relate to them throughout the whole adventure. The resonance in classic monster movies is to face 'the beast' - and that means creating a beast worth facing. I tend to group horror films into two categories - films about things that can exist and films about more fantastic things that cannot. Although The Blob is in the latter category, I did not want the creature to be so fantastic that it would have no connection with reality and thus provide no genuine threat. It had to be predictable to a certain extent. If there were a Blob, how would it behave? I wanted a creature that was a combination of the sensible and the fantastic.\" Effects for the film were approached from three different directions. Dream Quest Images - with whom Russell had worked on Nightmare on Elm Street III and Back to Schoolwas charged with creating miniature and optical effects. Makeup designer Tony Gardner - who served with Rick Baker and Greg Cannom on such projects as Cocoon, Harry and the Hendersons and Gorillas in the Mist - was given the job of creating special makeup effects for the many Blob victims. Design and construction of the Blob itself, however, was a task that fell to Lyle Conway - the Oscar-nominated mastermind behind the ingenious Audrey II plants in Little Shop of Horrors. Brought in some weeks later to coordinate the activities of all three was visual effects production supervisor Michael Fink, who had helmed the effects work on Wargames and The Adventures of Buckaroo Banzai. Having sought inspiration by watching footage of ameba, paramecia, men-of-war and jellyfish moving and ingesting food, Russell presented Lyle Conway and company with specific requirements for his title character. Though the very nature of the Blob demanded an essentially characterless creature, Russell wanted this Blob to have a threatening 'edge' not present in the article by original. To give it something other than a strictly amorphous Robert G. Pielke quality, Conway developed an entity with tentacles which would"],["52","The Blob","In The Blob - an updated basement classic - director Chuck Russell orchestrated involving some of the top talent in the field. Dream responsible for optical and miniature effects. Included among them were scenes of the blazing meteorite that delivers the Blob to earth. For a dramatic introductory covered with pyrotechnic from a crane, then elevated, directly over the camera. / A similarly obtained by sliding the fiery orb down a wire. / First to discover the Blob is an old can collector (Billy engulfed in a searing mass of malevolent goo. / Makeup Among the most grisly was the dissolved body of the can man who is consumed by the Blob while awaiting remake of the bargain an effects extravaganza Quest Images - under visual effects supervisor Hoyt Yeatman - was shot, a plexiglass ball material was suspended ignited and released in a giant arc that passed subsequent shot was Beck) whose hand is effects for the film were created by Tony Gardner. medical attention. 52 * CINEFEX 36 be operated by puppeteers - similar to the 'vine' technology used in Little Shop. Because of its ever-changing size and the various actions it would be called upon to perform - compounded by the fact that it would be employed in both miniature and live-action situations - numerous Blob puppets had to be constructed throughout the production schedule. Essentially, each puppet was fashioned from pieces of pre-dyed silk that were sewn together - somewhat like a quilt - to form random sized pouches two to four inches wide. Blood veins were airbrushed on; and immediately before shooting, the quilt was injected with methylcellulose - the effects industry standard for slime which was originally developed as a thickening agent for such diverse products as shampoos and milkshakes. \"We went through hundreds of gallons of methocel,\" noted visual effects supervisor Hoyt Yeatman. \"The stuff is primarily water - and water weighs a lot. Also it is very slippery and yet it dries like glue. As soon as we filled the pouches they started to ooze - so we constantly had to keep topping them off or else get new ones. It was kind of like trying to shoot ice cream \"Once methocel was mixed with water,\" added Fink, \"and made to kick into a gel with a small amount of ammonia, it was nearly impervious to cleanup. The gel would get stiffer with cold water, more slippery with hot water and would pretend to go away when hit with a steam cleaner - only to come back if you got the spot wet again. It stuck when it shouldn't have and it slid when you'd think it couldn't. It ate away at the asphalt parking lot, stripped paint from miniatures, gagged puppeteers and made slipping and falling standard procedure. If anything was close to Chuck Russell's intent for the Blob, it was the material from which it was made.\" As conceived and executed by Conway and crew, the various Blob manifestations were operated in a very direct way - primarily by puppeteers positioned underneath manipulating the forms with hands and feet and bodies. Unfortunately, the approach which had produced such splendid results in Little Shop of Horrors proved to be awkward and impractical during principal photography of The Blob. The extreme bulk of the methocel quilts - some weighing hundreds of pounds - made it difficult for the puppeteers to manage them in any controllable sense, especially through holes concealed in the set or even, on occasion, underwater. In the end, time and budget constraints both worked against the technique. \"Although we had done some preliminary tests with different materials and techniques,\" explained Yeatman, \"the Blob had not really been worked out totally by the time we started shooting. Nothing seemed just right. We had started with the concept of the right Blob for the right job' - meaning that we would make a certain type of Blob to perform a certain function - but because we were short on time, that eventually fell by the wayside. Once the live-action was well underway, we began getting ready to start miniature photography - but we found that the Blob just flat-out would not do what it was being asked to do. There"],["53","The Blob","CINEFEX 36 • 53"],["54","The Blob","would be a hole in the set and a puppeteer trying to move this big bag of goo - and it simply was not working.\" \"We would have liked to have had the time to work in front of video,\" added effects producer Keith Shartle, \"to have the creature effects people work out what they were going to do in advance. Had they had more lead time to really research and develop a wide variety of techniques, the photography would have gone much faster. But because the schedule was so tight, we were often receiving a Blob for a shot totally untried. So there we were, on the set, finding out for the first time what it could and could not do.\" As shooting progressed, Chuck Russell and his producers became increasingly displeased with the creature crew output, and for reasons presumably both practical and political decided to dismiss Lyle Conway immediately after the completion of principal photography. Brought in to replace him for the effects-heavy postproduction phase yet ahead was Stuart Ziff - architect of the go-motion system at ILM and ghost shop supervisor on Ghostbusters. \"The look and basic bag-of-goo technique for the Blob had been well established in the film and could not be changed,\" Fink commented. \"Stuart's background and knowledge of materials used for effects and his willingness to exploit expeditious techniques was invaluable. Stuart also brought in some other extraordinarily strong and talented people such as Gary Platek and Harold Weed. There were twenty-four people total on the Blob crew at the time of Lyle's departure, including office people. By the time we were at our postproduction peak, we had eighty people - about evenly split between puppeteers and fabricators.\" \"Originally there were not enough skilled puppeteers being used,\" Ziff stated. \"The people who were building the Blob were also being used to operate it - and that simply did not work. Nothing can substitute for having genuine talented puppeteers. One of the many significant things Mike Fink did was to hire thirty to thirty-five puppeteers with some film experience to operate the Blob.\" Even so, the weight problem was never adequately resolved. \"We struggled with shots sometimes for days,\" said Fink, \"not able to get what we wanted simply because our people - up to their eyebrows in methocel - could not lift the quilts.\" While hand-puppeteering would predominate, mechanical devices were employed when necessary. \"The Blob - like all puppets - was always more 'alive' when manipulated directly by hand, so we went to mechanical solutions only as a last resort. In some instances we used air bladders under the Blob to give it movement - mostly surface movement - where we were unable to get a hand or where there would have been too many hands. Also we could hook the bladders up in sequence to get a rippling effect - something that hands were unable to do because the puppeteers could not get the distance or the lift.\" Considerable effort was expended in developing a means to inflate and deflate the foam latex bladders quickly and with predictable results. \"If something has to be inflated fast,\" explained Ziff, \"you either have to have a very large hose and a 54 - CINEFEX 36"],["55","The Blob","small pressure differential or a small hose and a very large pressure differential. I used the former because using high pressures with a bladder can overfill and pop it. I designed a system using three-eighths-inch inside diameter hose and very large orifice valves. The valves would switch each bladder from vacuum to pressure and back again. The vacuum caused the bladder to deflate as quickly as it inflated. This was necessary because some of the scenes were shot at thirty-six or forty-eight frames per second which slowed down all the action. The valves were operated electrically by a microswitch keyboard - like a piano - which enabled the puppeteer to quickly and effortlessly make the bladders go up and down.\" The approach proved so successful that four of the keyboard devices - dubbed 'Ziff organs' by the crew - were fabricated, each capable of operating ten bladders. Though Fink and Ziff were able to make mechanical contributions, the actual look of the Blob had already been locked in, leaving little room for aesthetic alterations. Fink had particular problems resolving the design from a psychological perspective. \"The first week I was on the show, I met with Chuck and said: 'You have one major problem. The Blob - your central character - has no face, no arms, no eyes. How do we make it have intent?' That had never really been addressed. All of the puppeteers were saying, 'Give me a facel' Instead, all they had were tentacles - which were an attempt to give the Blob intent, but which I would have fought against if they had not already been established. Let the Blob extend itself, but no tentacles. The problem with the tentacles was integrating these mechanically articulated objects with the Blob itself. The two looks rarely, if ever, worked together. Also they were difficult to manipulate in a believable way. We could put short tentacles over hands and puppeteer them, but we did that in only a few instances and only when scale and desired movement allowed us to do so. For a majority of the shots, the tentacles had to be mechanized so that they could produce very fluid motion.\" With no alternative but to work within the limitations of the already-established design, the postproduction effects team devised methods that would make the ungainly Blob perform as needed. \"We shot in reverse whenever possible,\" Fink stated. \"We would put the particular tentacle in its ending position, then pull it away as fast as we could. When it was reversed on film, it looked like it was going right for the spot we wanted. It would have been very tough to get the tentacle to hit its mark any other way. The only problem with shooting in reverse was that the acceleration and deceleration curves were exactly reversed. So when you see the film run in reverse, the tentacle slows out to its stopping point because we were actually pulling it away. In reality, the slowest part of an accelerated movement is at the beginning. When shooting in reverse, you have to make things move really fast so the deceleration won't be noticeable - otherwise whatever you're filming looks like it's gliding to a stop.\" In addition to reverse photography, miniatures were employed when the weight of the various Blob manifestations rendered them unmanageable in full-scale. \"The live-action Blob could not get much beyond a two-by-three or three-by-five-foot slab,\" Yeatman explained. \"Anytime it had to move or writhe around or hit a specific mark, miniatures were used. Sometimes we had eighteen to twenty 'blobateers' working at one time just running one little two-foot Blob. We had to use video assists so that everyone could see what was happening.\" Unlike most miniature work where increased scale equates to increased credibility, the effects crew found that the smaller the Blob, the better it looked. Though a number of half-scale settings had already been constructed, subsequent ones were found to work better in eighth scale. While miniatures provided the only solution to handling the Blob's action for many of the scenes, the physical composition of the Blob itself presented a new set of difficulties in terms of generating opticals. \"The Blob was very slick and very shiny,\" explained Yeatman, \"which meant that we could not use traditional bluescreen and matte techniques. Those slimy, gooey sacks reflected light like a chrome ball - which made them terrible for matting - and yet they still had to be matted into the background somehow. Any kind of backlit screen - sodium vapor, front- and backlight or bluescreen - was deemed unsuitable because the Blob was so reflective. If we had tried to use time process photography - either Harryhausen-style stopmotion or what ILM calls 'go-motion' - combined with normal front or rear projection, we would have run into different problems. We were usually mixing film speeds, and process projectors have difficulties projecting back at high frame rates. Normally they cannot handle anything above twenty-four to thirty-six frames per second. Also the Blob was notorious for never hitting its mark - often we did forty or more takes before we got a piece of something that worked - so it would have been nearly impossible to try to synchronize Blob action with a background plate using normal front or rear projection.\" Any technique requiring motion control - such as the reverse bluescreen or ultraviolet matting approach - was also out of the question since the Blob action was virtually unrepeatable. \"The only real solution was to use front projected bluescreen. Since front projection employs a retro-reflective screen - where the background image is projected very faintly at the object and focused back intensely by the screen - the Blob could be photographed in front of such a screen without the kinds of spill problems that would have resulted from a traditionally backlit or frontlit material.\" Since the free-form puppets seemed to work best in small sizes, miniature settings had to be constructed for scenes in which the Blob had to appear large. The most significant was an eighth-scale reproduction of the entire town center of Abbeville, Louisiana - which doubled as the fictionalized ski resort town of Arborville - created by master modelmaker Greg Jein and his crew. Constructed at Dream Quest - where it would eventually be photographed - the model town measured thirty High school football hero Paul Taylor is the first Blob victim to be ingested on camera. The multistage effect required a variety of live and puppet elements in full-size and miniature configurations. Among the miniatures was a shot in which Taylor's girlfriend attempts to pull him from the gelatinous mass. Convincing representations of the characters were achieved via computer scanning of the actors and subsequent outputting of that data to a lathe device which manufactured wax replicas in third-scale. From these carvings it was possible for the makeup team to mold and cast a pair of articulated puppets. / A full-scale mechanical figure - actuated with cables and radio control - was employed for a final closeup of Taylor dissolving into the mass. Tony Gardner explains a detail of its operation to Chuck Russell. As developed by creature effects designer Lyle Conway early in the production, the various Blob forms were generally fashioned from pieces of dyed silk sewn together - much like a quilt - and then filled with methocel shortly before filming. CINEFEX 36 • 55"],["56","The Blob","Two teenagers in lovers' lane become the Blob's next victims. Unlike Paul Taylor and the old can collector, Vicki De Soto is consumed from within - essentially imploding as her insides are amorphous predator. Loren collapsing face. / Self-styled playboy Scott Jeskey (Ricky Paull Goldin) reacts with fear and revulsion as the girl he is attempting to seduce touch. / Tony Gardner and mechanical figure for its Fashioned from foam latex over an understructure of fiberglass and buterate, the Vicki puppet - actuated by cables and radio control - was made to collapse upon itself amidst an eruption eaten away by the Gitthens sculpts the literally shrivels at his Brian Wade prepare the grotesque performance. of stage blood and tentacles of Blob. 56 4 CINEFEX 36 feet by eighty feet. Meticulous preparation ensured that it would be an exact replica of the location site. \"A tremendous number of photographs were taken,\" noted Yeatman, \"documenting all the street signs and everything. Paint chips were taken off the buildings so that the colors in the model would match the liveaction.\" Because of the tight schedule, the miniatures had to be ready to shoot immediately after the location shooting wrapped. \"If we had shot the location part of the picture first,\" Shartle observed, \"and then had time to build the miniatures, we would have known what we were and were not going to need. Unfortunately, since we were to start shooting the miniatures immediately, we had to build everything - an entire city block - even though some of the miniature buildings and such would wind up never being on the screen.\" In shooting the miniatures - which would eventually have to cut in seamlessly with the live-action photography - Chuck Russell strove for lifelike realism. \"One of the dangers of miniature sets is that you can put the camera anywhere. I think it is wiser, however, to shoot at ground level and give yourself the same kinds of limitations you would have on a live-action set. If you do an impossible crane shot or an aerial point of view with a locked-off camera, chances are your miniature will look fake.\" Maintaining scale was equally crucial. Since fire was a predominant feature in the climactic battle sequence - and since actual flames in a miniature setting invariably look unrealistic - the decision was made to shoot full-size flame elements and composite them into the shots. \"We did a large number of open burns,\" said Yeatman. \"After shooting the miniature, we would go out to the parking lot at night and produce eight to ten fires which would then be combined with the miniature to help give justification for the lighting and to make it look full-scale.\" While miniatures were used extensively, little was required in the way of matte paintings. An exception was the opening shot. From the titles, the camera tilts down from the stars, through a cloud layer and finally to the earth - suggesting that the Blob is a visitor from another planet. Both the star background and the subsequent aerial shot of the town were paintings executed by matte supervisor Robert Scifo. \"As the camera breaks out of the clouds,\" Scifo explained, \"there is an overview of the town - apparently from the point of view of a helicopter. This aerial matte painting matches both the real town and the miniature - at least the main street area with the church and the theater and the park. The rest of the town was never really established, so it was invented based upon input from Chuck Russell as to what he thought Arborville should look like.\" Determined to add life to the painting, Scifo introduced a number of moving elements. \"We put in moving clouds and sparkling water. And since we were trying to make it look as if we were flying toward the town, we decided to add splitscreens to suggest a perspective shift. In the past, we have done at the most maybe three splits on a painting for a sky shot — one at the bottom, one halfway up and one at the top, We do that by masking off everything but one split - one section - at a time. Normally the bottom section will remain static. Then we mask off the bottom and the top in order to reveal the middle section. We drift that section - usually to the left or right - at a slow rate. Then we repeat the procedure at an increased rate for the top section. When all three are composited together with a soft-edged split, the sky appears to be moving faster at the top and slower at the bottom. With the Blob shot, however, we were moving towards a landscape - which tends to be less forgiving than clouds - so we had to have a lot more splits. We wound up using eleven splits on a painting that was only four by five feet, so every little section had to match exactly in density. Each one had to overlap to the right extent to keep it from getting either a double exposure or a dark area. We did that by placing a simple mechanical device on the matte stand in front of the lens. The device had an opening - a single window - which revealed one split at a time. Then we took the camera, which was on a motion control rig, and moved it toward the painting - at a fast rate for the bottom splits and slower for the top ones that were closer to the horizon. The overall effect was that the foreground was rushing toward camera and the background was standing still. To make it come alive even more, we added a roll axis to the camera to create simulated helicopter movement.\" Foreground clouds were matted in separately. Using one of the cavernous bays in their new facility in Simi Valley, Dream Quest technicians generated the clouds on a very large scale and shot them in a darkened environment. \"We heated the air in the room,\" Yeatman revealed, \"and then opened the shipping doors at night to get cold air in on the bottom and warm air on the top. This created an inversion layer. Then we used type A-B smoke - which is somewhat caustic and nasty to work with - to generate a cloud instantly that just sat there while the camera traveled through it for about fifty feet. Obviously that was something we could not have done in a cloud tank.\" As in the original film, the object rushing towards earth is soon revealed to be a meteorite. \"Joe Viskocil - who did our pyrotechnics - made a plexiglass ball which was one foot in diameter,\" Yeatman continued, \"and covered it in a magnesium type of powdered material. This we suspended with piano wire like a pendulum from a giant construction crane. A second crane carried the ball about a hundred feet up and out of frame. At nighttime, the ball was ignited with a propane torch and released. Just beneath the lowest part of the arc we set up the camera and turned it on at high speed. The result was a glowing orb with a ten foot tail of fire whizzing over the camera.\" The follow-on shot in which the meteor actually plummets to earth was executed in a similar way. \"The camera was stationed looking down a two-hundred-foot cliff and operated at high speed as the flaming ball slid past it on a single strand of wire. The resulting explosion was shot full-scale by Terry Frazee on a background plate that was later composited into the shot.\" The first encounter with the Blob occurs when an old can"],["57","The Blob","CINEFEX 36 • 57"],["58","The Blob","58 * CINEFEX 36"],["59","The Blob","collector comes upon the still smoldering crash site. Using a fallen tree branch to probe the crater, the man discovers a gelatinous mass clinging to the stick as he withdraws it. Suddenly the mass shoots up and engulfs his hand - one of the many Blob effects achieved by shooting in reverse. \"The Blob was placed on the end of the branch,\" Ziff explained, \"and someone reached his hand up from inside the crater. When the stick was withdrawn, the person in the crater pulled the mass off. Played back, it looks as if the Blob jumped onto the stick. This Blob was made of Aquapol - a polyurethane material that when mixed with water forms a very bizarre, Jello-like material that can be cast into any shape. A lot of care has to be taken when working with this stuff though, because the unreacted components are quite toxic.\" Horrified, the old man tries to escape the searing mass by chopping off his hand with a hatchet - a gag involving fake blood rigged to spurt out from concealed hoses. \"We had gallons of fake blood on the set, but ·5₴ Chuck decided he wanted still more. So we rigged more blood. Unfortunately, when we first tried it, there was air in the line and the stuff splattered all over everyone and everything.\" Discovered by Paul and Meg, the Blob's first victim is taken to a local hospital where he is placed on an examining table while awaiting medical attention. Before help can be administered, however, the Blob consumes the whole lower half of the old man's body - a particularly grisly scene devised by Tony Gardner and his crew. \"My idea was that the Blob was after flesh and blood - bones, fingernails and hair were really just waste material - so the victims would actually dissolve kind of bloodlessly. They would become smoother and more translucent until they were almost pearlescent. Chuck wanted the deaths to be bloodier, though - at least at first.\" For the ingested can man, Gardner attached a giant appliance to actor Billy Beck who was positioned on a platform beneath the surface of the examining table. \"It was a foam piece that blended onto his neck below the jaw line. His own head came up through the bottom of the table but the rest of his body was artificial, with a lace beard added to the appliance neck to help blend it to the actor. The majority of his bubbling 'guts' were sculpted into the foam and fiberglass appliance. We painted a layer of stain onto the material and dressed a layer of slime on top of that. Inside crevices sculpted into the appliance we layered some calcium turnings - small calcium pellets that look like gravel except they are silver colored. When water is added, the turnings generate intense heat and a lot of steam - a factor we took into consideration by sealing the false torso to the table so that we would not risk burning Billy if water leaked under the appliance.\" The Blob - now about five feet across - is loose in the hospital. Attempting to investigate, Paul Taylor is trapped by the loathsome creature and completely engulfed in its mass. Since a full-scale Blob of that size would have weighed hundreds of pounds, miniatures were used for scenes of the Blob slithering up the wall behind its victim. \"We shot Paul bluescreen,\" Yeatman recalled, \"then built a half-scale miniature of the doctor's office interior on a cant - about a twenty-five degree tilt. The camera was positioned to look down on it. Then one of the quilttype Blobs was rolled down the wall and shot in reverse so that when it was run forward it appeared to be slithering up. This was then put together with the bluescreen element of Paul in the foreground.\" The ingestion of Paul Taylor - the first on-screen example of the Blob feeding - employed three articulated puppets and a special full-size Blob rig for actor Donovan Leitch. It was also one of the most difficult assignments for the makeup crew - and in the end proved sufficiently effective that it needed to be trimmed for the film to avoid an X-rating. \"It was very graphic,\" conceded Gardner. \"There were closeups of his face decomposing and closeups of his arm being ripped off in slow motion and in great detail.\" The sequence begins with a view of Paul trapped inside the Blob, accomplished during postproduction with Leitch sitting on a rolling platform encased in a translucent pink fiberglass shell over which went layers of fabric in various colors and patterns. On top of all of this went several Blob quilts, each with a specific design and function. Though the actor had his own air supply, he had to hold his breath during each take - in the process getting generous doses of methocel up his nose, in his mouth and even in his ears. The next shot in the sequence - Meg running forward and grabbing Paul's hand - marked Tony Gardner's initiation into Blob effects as well as makeup, \"I was essentially drafted into doing the shot. I was told on the Wednesday after Lyle left the picture that this particular creature shot needed to work the following Tuesday morning: and since I had shared a shop with Lyle and i he est one was aware of what was being used, that I would be the best one to handle it. The rig we built was a rolling cart with a heatformed back shell supported by a post on one end and the actor's back on the other. That way Donovan could puppeteer it and the back would not appear so rigid. Over that went two layers of clear vinyl bladders, an Aquapol 'shroud' to keep the puppeteer fairly dry and then layers of spandex and nylon tricot fabric cut into vein patterns. On top of all of this went a tailored Blob quilt jacket' with blue streaks painted into it and some underlying layers of fabric in various shades of red. When we pulled the layers back off his body at different speeds and from different directions, we would get flashes of purple as well as various shades of red and blue. It really created a sense of activity within the Blob as it digested Paul.\" Next came a shot of Meg attempting to pull Paul out of the gooey mass. \"That we accomplished with third-scale mechanical puppets of the two characters, although we only had to concentrate on Donovan from the waist up. For accuracy, we had a company called Image Masters do a computer scan of Donovan's head and shoulders and reduce that image to the scale we needed. Then their computer fed the data into a lathe which duplicated his form three-dimensionally in carving wax. From the wax copy we were able to resculpt, mold and cast what we needed. Next we used another third-scale mechanical puppet The Blob next emerges from a sink in the local diner where it leaps onto the cook and quickly sucks him headfirst down the drain. For the next scene - in which the bloated mass explodes out of the sink and onto the ceiling - a half-scale section of the set was constructed upside-down at Dream Quest. Then methocel Blob material was pumped through the drain while an inverted camera recorded the action at high-speed. / Makeup effects crew members Andrew Kenworthy and Christian Wabschall examine a pair of feet made from hot-melt vinyl - the last vestiges of the cook as he vanishes down the drain. / Though most of the Blob forms were puppeteered manually, a few particularly troublesome ones required mechanical devices. À pneumatically activated Blob - designed by Stuart Ziff - is photographed by Hoyt Yeatman using a frontprojected bluescreen technique to avoid blue spill from the highly reflective subject. CINEFEX 36 • 59"],["60","The Blob","Trapped inside a telephone booth, waitress Fran Hewitt (Candy Clark) collapses in gnef and fear as she spots sheriff floating within the away. To achieve the shot, a booth and filled with Blob material. Submerged amidst the methocel quilts was a partially consumed lawman. Skinned with foam latex and fabrics and resins to create a mechanically via cables and projectionist fails to make a manager (Pons Maar) enters the booth to investigate - only to find the remains of his yo-yo tossing employee embedded in a mass of Blob the remains of the town gooey mass only inches large aquarium was constructed around the puppet representing the covered with translucent dissolution effect, the puppet was maneuvered from behind by a scuba diver and actuated radio control. / When the Blob invades the local theater it leaves behind a fallen cheerleader whose face has melted into the carpet - another puppet creation by Gardner and company. / When the reel change, the theater material on the ceiling. 60 4 CINEFEX 36 sculpted in a more advanced state of liquefaction. At this point, the scene begins cutting to inserts of Paul's arm being pulled off. Chuck had been very specific about this. ile wanted the arm to look like bubblegum stretching until it snapped, so we made tendons and veins out of latex, spandex and nylon and put them inside a mechanism in the arm stump that would release each strand at a different time.\" When we cut back to the last closeup of Paul as he dissolves, we are actually looking at a head that was sculpted by Brian Wade from a mold of the actor's head and shoulders. The jaw is stretched down to mid-chest level and continues on out of frame. His eyebags sag, his face thins out and blood starts pouring from his eyes and mouth. All of the larger movements were operated by cables, while the eyes were radio-controlled.\" After consuming Paul, the Blob finds a couple in a lovers' lane and devours them also. A slightly different death scene was devised for the girl who essentially implodes as the Blob rises through her body and dissolves her from the inside out. \"Her head completely caves in and tentacles shoot out of her face and chest,\" said Gardner. \"The effect starts with the actress turning her head. Once the turn was underway, her real head was replaced by a puppet - essentially a foam latex skin glued over a collapsing understructure of fiberglass and buterate. Separate pieces of the skull were connected to different cables so that they could be pulled in at different times. The eyes were rigged to roll up together. As her face was pulled in and blood was pumped out, the camera cut to another angle - to a head that was sculpted by Loren Gitthens in the final collapsed state, with some very simple mechanics under the skin to make the surface bubble and undulate. Track tubing was used to actuate the tentacles coming out of her chest.\" The creature continues its rampage in the local diner - erupting from the sink, grabbing the cook and pulling him down the drain. The Blob that bursts out of the drain was a sculpted piece - about two-and-a-half feet in diameter - that consisted of a clear body and a heavily-veined 'foreskin' piece that traveled up and over the main body surface as it attached itself to the cook's face. The initial shot was done in reverse, with the Blob tendril being pulled off actor Clayton Landey and into the sink. From there the sequence continued with a stuntman being yanked into the sink by a firmly attached tendril. A high-angle shot next affords a view of the cook's Blobengulfed head dissolving into the drain - accomplished with a false head that deflated like a balloon while the stuntman thrashed around, his real head concealed beneath the counterfeit in a cutaway section of the sink. Within seconds, all that is left of the cook are several stray limbs spinning and thrashing their way down the drain. The two feet sticking out were cast from hot-melt vinyl because of its translucent quality and its ability to compress rather than fold or wrinkle as foam rubber would. Crew member Christian Wabschall was placed on a giant lazy susan - affectionately dubbed the 'Spin and Barf - to affect the twisting hand movements in the air. Two puppeteers were also in attendance to operate the legs and control the flow of steam and blood. In tight closeup, the last body part to be sucked down the drain was a foam latex deflatable foot manA few moments later the drain explodes - belching a giant column of Blob matter straight up and onto the ceiling. From that overhead position, the undulating mass chases Brian and Meg down a hallway to a walk-in freezer where the terrified teenagers seek shelter. \"We shot the Blob bursting out of the sink high-speed and upside-down using a two-hundred-gallon dump tank,\" explained Yeatman. \"The next shot is a low-angle that pans up to spot the Blob hitting the ceiling and taking out one of the fluorescent lamps. For that we built a half-scale set - also upside-down. We used a combination of a dump tank and a core piece with tentacles that would fling out when it hit the ceiling.\" The follow-on shot of the Blob pursuing Meg and Brian had been unsuccessfully attempted during principal photography. In postproduction, Eric Allard and his All Effects Group - creators of the Short Circuit robot - devised an air cart to which the nearly four-hundred-pound Blob quilt was fitted. \"The foreground with the two actors was shot against a traditional twenty-by-forty-foot transmission bluescreen and the background was a half-scale miniature - shot high-speed and upside-down by a camera mounted on a monorail system. Inside the air cart sat Chris Emmett - one of the Blob shop crew members - outfitted with scuba gear. The whole thing was on a dolly system that employed air bearings and wheels. That Blob was photographed separately and later combined with a bluescreen element of Meg and Brian shot running on a treadmill.\" After reaching the freezer, the Blob tries to force its way in to get at the terrified teenagers. A little tongue of slime slithers under the door but promptly crinkles up and pulls away when it touches some ice - a foreshadowing of the creature's eventual demise. \"We used hot-melt vinyl for the tongue,\" Ziff noted, \"hardened with Crazy Glue and painted red. Inside was a levered mechanism that would stretch the vinyl - flat fingerlike extensions that would push out the end. Since the surface was very brittle, it would crack exposing a white subsurface. When it withdrew, it left little crystals behind on the floor - which we just laid underneath beforehand. The accompanying smoke was achieved by pouring liquid nitrogen.\" Witness to the horror, waitress Fran Hewitt runs outside where she unwisely stops at a telephone booth to call the town sheriff for help. Unsuccessful, she instead becomes the Blob's next victim as the now gargantuan mass engulfs the tiny structure. \"All Effects made a half-scale phone booth about four feet tall,\" Ziff related. \"We set up six air mortars packed with Blob material and all of them were pointed at the miniature for the big attack. When they fired, though, it was just too much - we couldn't see anything clearly - so eventually we wound up using only two.\" Moments before she succumbs, Fran sees the dissolving form"],["61","The Blob","CINEFEX 36 • 61"],["62","The Blob","of the sheriff already inside the creature. For the sequence, a large aquarium was built around the full-size phone booth. A puppet representing the half-consumed sheriff was then submerged inside where it was to continue disintegrating. \"We experimented with a lot of things like gelatins and Bromo Seltzer to get the dissolving action,\" Gardner commented, \"but we found that the action itself tended to obscure the very things we wanted to see. We realized that we had to come up with something that was controllable and repeatable - and yet clear enough to see through. We experimented in a big industrial sink first. Next we went to a fish tank with a small version of our sheriff puppet, then a bigger fish tank and finally to the actual tank built for the scene.\" Though Gardner and his crew had built several bodies for the scene, only the sheriff's face actually made it into the film. \"As the body first appears, it hits the glass of the phone booth straight on and then its half-eaten face angles into view - the mouth stretching out, the face caving in and the eyes moving all the while. To create the impression of skin streaming off his face, we dressed the foam latex skin with elements that would appear translucent in water - a flexible resin, nylon, some hair net material and some other lightweight and sheer fabrics. We painted veins in very light patterns with an airbrush. I wanted the effect to have the look of milk or colored liquid being poured into water - not chunks of hamburger breaking away The puppet was articulated by cables attached to levers and by radio-controlled servo motors placed outside the tank. \"We also had two puppeteers in scuba gear actually in the tank with the puppet for a minimal amount of hand puppeteering. Bill Sturgeon had made the understructure for the puppet in such a way that we could easily submerge it in water without damaging the mechanics. The scuba divers were directly behind the puppet - Chris Goehe operated the mouth and body position while the other puppeteered the Blob quilt submerged in the water, parting it so the puppet could make its entrance.\" The sheriff's deputy had an equally grim demise. Attempting to secure the town hall where many of the townsfolk have sought refuge, Bill Briggs barricades the door with a large bookshelf. Suddenly a Blob tentacle explodes through from the back of the bookcase, wraps itself around the deputy and proceeds to pull him through the opening it had just made - in the process, snapping him in half at the waist. \"Todd Chaney took the actual bookcase that was going to be used on the set.\" said Gardner, \"and created a new base for it that would tip it back at an angle but still support it enough so that we could add onto it. The next step was the addition of a long 'table' off the back side of the bookcase. On that table was a small sliding board - like a sled - on which actor Paul McCrane would sit so that his legs extended through the opening in the shelf. Working from a fiberglass body cast of Paul, we manufactured a pair of mechanical legs. Meanwhile, Teresa Burkett fabricated a harness that would attach the fake legs to Paul's waist while he was sitting on the sliding board and give the audience the 62 • CINEFEX 36"],["63","The Blob","impression that the legs he was 'standing' on were his own. Two technicians pulled the sled across the platform while a puppeteer operated the knees and ankles of the false legs. With a breakaway shelf and back wall, plus some lightweight prop books for dressing, all Paul had to do was pull his arms in and lay down - the rest was up to us.\" The one major sequence retained from the original film begins in the theater projection booth where projectionist Phil Hobbs is sucked up by what became known as the 'Bubblegum Blob' - a foam latex sculpture affixed to the ceiling and fitted with bladders and six-foot mechanical tentacles. \"The Hobbs puppet was created from a full body cast of actor Frank Collison. A fiberglass replica was manufactured, and the body - as well Cute over as the area of the Blob around it - was sculpted over this by Evan Brainard and Mike Smithson. After the sculpture was molded and cast in urethane foam, the fiberglass was cut into sections and mechanized. Since the Hobbs figure was supposed to be capable of releasing his yo-yo during the shot, areas were left in the sculpture so that the projectionist's arms would actually be those of the puppeteers operating the head and chest movement. The head was radio-controlled by two operators - one on either side of the camera. In all, more than fifteen people were engaged to actuate the various bladders, tentacles and body movements required for the shot. After engulfing the projectionist, the Blob attacks the audience seated in the theater. The sequence was achieved by combining bluescreen elements and miniatures. \"In order to overcome the look of a miniature,\" explained electronics supervisor Fred Iguchi, \"it is fairly typical to use high-speed photography. This, in turn, almost always involves combining separate film elements shot at different times, places and speeds, In order for all these pieces to match perfectly, the lighting for each element has to be duplicated exactly. We needed a way to create interactive lighting, so we put together a computer system that had eight different channels for lights.\" The eight-channel microprocessor - devised by Iguchi and dubbed the 'Blinkatron' - was especially useful in the unique lighting environment of the movie theater. \"We wanted the sparks and flashes created by the Blob in the projection room to create a strobe effect,\" stated Yeatman. \"Since we were shooting separate film elements, we had to have a way of duplicating the apparently random flashes that occurred in the live-action when we later shot the bluescreen and miniature elements. The Blinkatron linked up with both the production camera and our own Vistavision plate camera.\" Since the light was supposedly being emitted from a broken projector, a random strobe pattern was devised and maintained throughout the sequence - the special effects shots as well as the live-action. \"The lighting sequence was created on location,\" Iguchi elaborated, \"and modified until the director liked it. Then that was saved on a hard disc to be retrieved when another film element was shot. The computer was slaved to the shutter of the camera so that no matter what kind of lighting we used, the only time a flash of light could occur was when the shutter was wide open. All of our cameras interfaced with this computer which could proceed at any speed - one frame per second or a hundred frames per second - so when we shot our miniatures we were able to duplicate the lighting sequence exactly by playing back the disc. Of course, the intensity and placement of the lighting had to match as well - the shadows, for example, had to be going in the same direction.\" A Vistavision plate camera was set up in the theater and directed across a set of seats to the background where frantic moviegoers were scrambling to escape. \"We marked off with tape where the Blob was going to be - in the midground on the seats,\" said Yeatman. \"Then we had Shawnee Smith come through and act as if she were seeing the Blob. This was shot as just a reference take. The actual production footage of the background was essentially an empty frame, except for the people scrambling around as the lights blinked and flickered in accordance with the preapproved pattern.\" Months later, the theater set was replicated in one-sixth scale - a scale dictated by some articulated one-sixth-scale theatergoer puppets already built. \"In shooting the miniature, we placed the strobes in the appropriate places, recalled the lighting pattern from the disc and shot the Blob high-speed and in reverse. The lights also played at high-speed and in reverse and were perfectly synchronized with the background. Still later, we did the same thing SERGER with the Blinkatron on the bluescreen stage, shooting Shawnee and other people running around in front of her in the foreground. Ultimately, we wound up with three film elements all cohesively blended together.\" When Meg enters the auditorium of the theater she is knocked to the floor and almost lands on top of one of her cheerleading pals. Meg turns her over and discovers that the girl's face is sliding off and has become attached to the floor. The 'melted cheerleader' was actually a mechanically assisted dummy constructed by the makeup effects crew. \"It had basically a loosejointed body,\" Gardner revealed, \"with a cable-operated head and neck. We tried to cast the head up in Aquapol, but the stuff did not want to stay in the same configuration it was cast in - which was great for the melting half, but not for the side of her face that was supposed to look unharmed. We ended up having to cast it out of standard latex and polyfoam. The tendons were latex, spandex and nylon glued to her head and the carpeting. Calcium turnings were used again for a bubbling and steaming effect.\" After the debacle at the theater, the Blob crawls into the underground sewer system, trapping Meg along with her little brother and his friend Eddie. This Blob was the only fullymechanized puppet used in the film. As ultimately filmed, Eddie falls victim to the awful pursuer while Meg and her brother watch in horror. Eddie is sucked down into the swirling waistdeep water which then goes quiet and still. Suddenly, however, he bursts forth - encased in slime and slowly dissolving. \"Since it was basically a shock cut - there and gone in a moment - Technicians and puppeteers wrestle with a large Blob quilt between takes in the sewer set. Only rarely in the film was a full-size Blob employed - primarily because of the extreme heaviness of the methocel. For the most part either halfor eighth-scale versions were used - puppeteered by hand in miniature settings or against blue for later optical compositing. / Fleeing through the sewer, Eddie Beckner is overtaken by the Blob and sucked underwater. Moments later - to the horror of his companions - he struggles to the surface, already covered with slime and partially consumed. Tony Gardner attends to final details on the foam rubber suit wor by stuntman Bobby Porter during the scene. CINEFEX 36 • 63"],["64","The Blob","Bursting up through the pavement, the Blob wreaks residents flee in terror and military troops launch an ineffectual counteroffensive. A meticulous eighth-scale constructed at Dream Quest by master modelmaker Greg scene, a seven-foot pillar of methocel and dropped onto the miniature road surface where it crushed a car and a small human figure. / Stage hands prepare for a shot in which the Blob invades the sensitive to gravity, most scenes of it climbing were shot in reverse - often over miniatures placed at a cant. / In another model shot, a snowmaking truck plows into the alien juggernaut. havoc on Arborville as replica of the town was Jein and crew. For one Blob was hoisted into position, doused with town hall. Since the methocel quilts were 64 → CINEFEX 36 we did not have to worry about the character melting before our eyes,\" Gardner commented. \"As a result, we designed and built a foam rubber suit that stuntman Bobby Porter could get in and out of fairly rapidly - except for the head, which was glued down around his eyes and mouth. The suit included remnants of melted clothing as well as a 'Blob cape' which attached to his shoulders and 'Blob tendons' which ran from his arms down to a submersed platform where a technician kept tension on the other end. The idea was to create the illusion that Eddie was straining against a large mass that was trying to pull him under. The actor had an air supply and was able to stay underwater comfortably until some air mortars behind him blew - his signal to explode off the platform.\" Meg and her brother are saved by Brian who helps them climb up to street level where government forces have moved in to contain the beast. Shortly thereafter, the Blob bursts out of the sewers into the center of town - a dramatic entrance achieved in miniature with mechanisms devised by Phil Bartko. \"The miniature set was placed about twelve feet in the air,\" said Yeatman. \"Just under the tarmac road surface was a series of pie-shaped doors which opened up at 'ground zero.' Underneath the set was a giant air mortar - a cylinder about twenty-four inches in diameter and eight feet tall with a plunger at the bottom. Sculpted onto the plunger was a rigid fiberglass piece of the Blob. Around that we poured several hundred gallons of red methocel. Then the pie-shaped doors were closed and dressed with road surface. We also had steam lines and air cannons to fire debris when the Blob broke through. At the appropriate time, all of this was fired off in front of our frontprojected bluescreen. The resulting footage was later composited with the live-action, which included a lot of dramatic stunt work. All of this was about thirty-nine frames long - weeks and months of work for a second-and-a-half of film!\" To the dismay of the Abbeville residents who gathered nightly to watch their home town being transformed into a military battle zone, the Blob itself never made an appearance. All the street scenes involving it were done in miniature. Stuart Ziff and his crew devised a means for the Blob to be 'struck' by miniature gunfire. \"We created this wonderful device whereby we pulled little plugs out from the bottom of a table to make it look like the Blob was being hit by bullets. The table was about six feet square and about chest high. A Blob quilt was placed on top with holes sewn into it about two inches in diameter. Plugs were put into each hole with a little silk cover and dressed to look smooth. Eight puppeteers stood around the table with their arms under the quilt. When the director gave the cue to fire, a guy underneath the table pulled a rod that all the plugs were attached to and they literally pulled through the bottom leaving holes in the quilt. The puppeteers then 'mushed' the quilt with their hands causing the holes to fuse together again as if they were immediately healing over.\" Smoke and debris were also incorporated as the Blob's enormous form moved down the street. \"At one point,\" remarked Yeatman, \"the Blob does a 'belly flop' on the pavement - six hundred pounds of Blob material arching up and landing flat on the deck, breaking up the ground surface. We did that on a miniature set that had a neoprene road surface with wedgeshaped pieces of wood on tracks underneath the table. So when the Blob came down, it was synced up with cables and pulleys and cams to push out the wood blocks under the table, breaking up the outer road surface. This gave the impression of the road being depressed by something extremely heavy.\" Puppet animation enhanced with motion control was also employed to articulate one of the Blob's tentacles as it smashes down on a victim, instantly absorbing him. \"The technique combined hand animation and go-motion using a motorized device to give some blur between frames. If we had suddenly thrown a completely animated shot into the composite it would have looked like animation. We did use some straight animation for the articulation of the edges of the tentacle and for the shadow that falls across the man as the tentacle is raised, but the main action was done with the motion control system. The tentacle had to hit its mark very accurately and then pull back to reveal that the man had been absorbed, so motion control was really the best choice.\" The overall effect was completed with a plate of a stuntman falling. As the tentacle slapped down, the live figure was 'popped off optically and replaced by an embedded puppet visible when the tentacle raised back up. In filming the battle sequence, Russell wanted the freedom to shoot the Blob at whatever film speed he felt was appropriate to achieve a given effect. \"To do that,\" said Iguchi, \"he could have changed the f-stop on the camera as the speed changed - but then the depth of field would have changed also, so that was not a viable method. What we did was design and build a circular 'fader glass' with graduated levels of neutral density material that rotated in front of the camera. With a microprocessor slaved to the camera to detect the shutter speed, the glass could be turned to vary the amount of light reaching the lens without actually changing the lights themselves. In other words, if we were running the camera at twice sound speed, we would have to have twice as much light. But with this system, all we had to do was light for the maximum and let the neutral density wheel take it down a stop or more to compensate for the extra light and the slower shutter speed. Using this device, we were able to track film speeds from six frames per second all the way up to 120 frames per second - and it was all done in real time using this tiny microprocessor.\" The device was built in only two weeks, with Iguchi, designer Robert Wilcox and technician Joseph Hagey working around the clock to complete it in time. Though it proved extremely effective, none of the shots that employed the fader glass were ultimately included in the film. In the editing process, purely by happenstance, Russell chose shots that had been done without a change in the camera speed. Crucial to the climactic sequence was an overturned snowmaking machine that would ultimately spell doom for the cold-"],["65","The Blob","CINEFEX 36 • 65"],["66","The Blob","66 CINEFEX 36"],["67","The Blob","sensitive Blob. At one point in the action, the cab of the vehicle - completely engulfed by the juggernaut - was to have provided a bizarre vantage point for a closeup view of ingested bodies floating by. \"We went to a swimming pool at an apartment complex for our video tests,\" Gardner recalled. \"We rented scuba gear and took our two full-body puppets - basically marionettes - that were controlled by rods and monofilament. They had to float by a very small window surface, each from an opposite direction, immersed in Blob material. It took us about a week-and-a-half to work out the puppeteering for the sequence because we were dealing with neutral buoyancy and a small space. After we had the sequence worked out, it was discovered that the tank that was built to actually accomplish the shot was not reinforced properly. It kept fracturing and finally blew out its bottom - so the shot was cut.\" During the final battle, Reverend Meeker is severely burned by a stray flame thrower burst. Not only did Gardner have to create an authentic-looking burn for actor Del Close, he also had to formulate the resulting scar for the film's epilogue which occurs two years later. \"We consulted with a plastic surgeon - Stanley P. Frileck in Beverly Hills - to find out what this kind of burn would look like originally and also what it would look like two years later. He let us research photographs and manuals and also consulted with Mike Smithson who was sculpting the piece so that we could make Del's burn makeup as accurate as possible. Most of our makeup effects on the film could look like anything we felt was correct and we could justify our interpretation by saying, That's what someone looks like when he's blobbed.' But everyone knows what burns look like, so they had to be convincing.\" The film ends with Meeker - scarred both physically and mentally - preaching at a tent meeting, warning the assemblage of the Judgment soon to come. During his sermon, the clergyman holds up a jar containing a small quivering piece of the Blob - a souvenir collected from the frozen creature's crystallized remains years before. The effect was achieved by Gary Platek who constructed and puppeteered a prosthetic arm and hand with a wire system inside to actuate the bottled Blob sample. \"We shot a plate of Del Close holding a jar,\" said Yeatman, \"and then had him lower it out of the shot. At that point his arm was replaced with the prosthetic arm holding the cable puppet inside the jar - which was combined with the live-action by filming it in front of a rear-projected plate of the reverend.\" Burdened with budget limitations, a tight schedule, personnel changes and a leading character that was, in essence, an uncooperative bag of goo, Chuck Russell nonetheless found the experience of remaking The Blob a joyful one. \"It was very hectic. There was not the usual feeling of satisfaction that you get on a 'normal' film once principal photography wraps, because as a special effects film we were just getting started at that point. All of the opticals and composites were coming together until the very last possible moment. It was crazy. In spite of that, though, I have never had more fun doing a film.\" Dressed in black for maximum concealment, puppeteers position one of the Blob forms for a scene with the overturned snow truck. During the take they will be hidden beneath the set, manipulating the Blob by hand through holes cut into the miniature pavement. / Exhausted from working beneath one of the quilts, puppeteer Howard Stallings emerges for a breath of air between takes. / Deputy Bill Briggs is killed when the Blob grabs him around the waist and pulls him bodily through a bookcase - virtually snapping him in half in the process. To achieve the desired illusion, a special harness with false legs was constructed for actor Paul McCrane. / Seated on the shelf with his own legs straight out in front, the actor was simply grasped by the ankles and yanked feetfirst through the opening - a maneuver which forced the false legs to pivot upward at a back-breaking angle. / For an epilogue set two years after the main events in the film, makeup artist Mike Smithson solicited guidance from a prominent plastic surgeon regarding the production of a convincing two-year-old burn scar for actor Del Close. CINEFEX 36 • 67"],["68","Closing pages",""]]});
